1
00:00:01,207 --> 00:00:02,306
Let's go.

2
00:00:02,307 --> 00:00:03,747
(The Fiery Father and Son)

3
00:00:04,027 --> 00:00:06,597
(MINHO goes into a building.)

4
00:00:07,077 --> 00:00:09,447
- It looks like a college building. - I think he's going to exercise.

5
00:00:09,747 --> 00:00:11,517
He should do activities other than just working out.

6
00:00:14,687 --> 00:00:17,857
I can't do anything since they're in the middle of their training.

7
00:00:20,657 --> 00:00:21,796
He's at a soccer field.

8
00:00:21,797 --> 00:00:23,896
(The soccer training is in full swing.)

9
00:00:23,897 --> 00:00:25,137
You don't have to rush out.

10
00:00:27,037 --> 00:00:29,237
Seung Joon, you don't have to rush that play just now.

11
00:00:29,507 --> 00:00:31,207
Get away!

12
00:00:33,446 --> 00:00:34,476
It's overpowering.

13
00:00:35,213 --> 00:00:36,353
He sounds intense.

14
00:00:36,747 --> 00:00:38,007
I can't approach him.

15
00:00:40,647 --> 00:00:42,287
(Hesitant)

16
00:00:42,947 --> 00:00:44,117
He seems scared.

17
00:00:45,087 --> 00:00:46,717
He's very nervous.

18
00:00:47,887 --> 00:00:49,587
Just wait. Hold the spot there.

19
00:00:50,357 --> 00:00:52,027
Wait. Go back!

20
00:00:53,297 --> 00:00:54,457
Wait there.

21
00:00:56,127 --> 00:00:57,227
Salute!

22
00:00:57,567 --> 00:00:58,637
That was sudden.

23
00:00:59,167 --> 00:01:01,137
I've known him since middle school.

24
00:01:01,907 --> 00:01:03,336
(Who is he?)

25
00:01:03,337 --> 00:01:05,137
He's the division commander Yeonsu-gu, Incheon.

26
00:01:07,837 --> 00:01:08,907
Is it over?

27
00:01:10,277 --> 00:01:12,617
(While looking around the field, he carefully steps forward.)

28
00:01:13,317 --> 00:01:15,187
Dad.

29
00:01:17,247 --> 00:01:20,087
(He was MINHO's dad?)

30
00:01:20,917 --> 00:01:21,957
Dad.

31
00:01:23,887 --> 00:01:25,357
They have the same eyes.

32
00:01:25,627 --> 00:01:27,826
You have his eyes and nose.

33
00:01:27,827 --> 00:01:29,366
Min Seok does resemble his dad.

34
00:01:29,367 --> 00:01:30,367
(Min Seok is MINHO's brother.)

35
00:01:30,368 --> 00:01:32,567
My father and his father are close friends too.

36
00:01:32,837 --> 00:01:34,137
Our families are quite close.

37
00:01:35,467 --> 00:01:37,367
I see. You already know each other.

38
00:01:38,137 --> 00:01:40,077
- When did you get here? - About 30 minutes ago.

39
00:01:40,237 --> 00:01:41,237
I see.

40
00:01:41,238 --> 00:01:44,107
I'll be done in ten minutes.

41
00:01:44,877 --> 00:01:47,117
- He's sweet to his son. - What a sweet dad.

42
00:01:48,047 --> 00:01:51,487
My father is the head coach of Yongin FC in K League Two.

43
00:01:51,787 --> 00:01:53,657
His name is Choi Yun Kyum.

44
00:01:53,817 --> 00:01:56,627
He's been coaching for over 20 years.

45
00:01:56,827 --> 00:01:59,997
He's the oldest head coach in the Korean soccer industry.

46
00:02:00,127 --> 00:02:01,926
- For 20 years? - He's a veteran coach.

47
00:02:01,927 --> 00:02:02,967
My gosh.

48
00:02:03,167 --> 00:02:04,667
He's an experienced coach...

49
00:02:04,997 --> 00:02:06,837
who's gone through all kinds of hardships.

50
00:02:08,137 --> 00:02:09,707
(With years of experience playing professionally...)

51
00:02:09,967 --> 00:02:11,977
(and representing the national team at the 1988 Seoul Summer Games,)

52
00:02:13,307 --> 00:02:17,646
(he transitioned into coaching.)

53
00:02:17,647 --> 00:02:18,647
He's so cool.

54
00:02:18,648 --> 00:02:21,377
MINHO was bound to be athletic.

55
00:02:21,547 --> 00:02:24,587
MINHO must have gotten good athletic genes from him.

56
00:02:25,717 --> 00:02:27,056
(Coached and trained Bucheon SK, coached Daejeon Hana Citizen FC,)

57
00:02:27,057 --> 00:02:28,526
(coached Caykur Rizespor, coached Hoang Anh Gia Lai FC...)_

58
00:02:28,527 --> 00:02:29,987
He's the legend of K League.

59
00:02:31,357 --> 00:02:34,997
(The first coach of Yongin FC, Choi Yun Kyum)

60
00:02:35,827 --> 00:02:37,526
Yongin FC was founded quite recently.

61
00:02:37,527 --> 00:02:41,497
As his son, I wanted to show him my support.

62
00:02:41,746 --> 00:02:43,746
I was also curious how he was doing with the team.

63
00:02:43,937 --> 00:02:45,437
So I went to the field for a visit.

64
00:02:46,577 --> 00:02:48,177
- I'll be done in ten minutes. - Okay.

65
00:02:48,977 --> 00:02:50,976
- Should I... Okay. - I'll come back later.

66
00:02:50,977 --> 00:02:52,147
- You should go. - Okay.

67
00:02:52,917 --> 00:02:54,047
- You got this. - Okay.

68
00:02:55,017 --> 00:02:58,317
Keep running. You have to keep running once you're in midfield.

69
00:02:59,057 --> 00:03:00,787
Go back quickly.

70
00:03:01,487 --> 00:03:03,827
You must get out of it. You'll get surrounded right away.

71
00:03:04,757 --> 00:03:06,497
Just wait. Hold the spot there.

72
00:03:07,227 --> 00:03:08,367
Wait!

73
00:03:09,667 --> 00:03:13,037
(MINHO always showed his fiery charisma while playing sports.)

74
00:03:14,537 --> 00:03:16,037
- He's just like his dad. - I can see the resemblance.

75
00:03:16,467 --> 00:03:18,007
- There's a resemblance. - The athletic genes.

76
00:03:18,207 --> 00:03:20,336
MINHO is fiery like a flame.

77
00:03:20,337 --> 00:03:22,407
Then his father is explosive like a volcano.

78
00:03:23,307 --> 00:03:25,977
I think his father has more explosive energy.

79
00:03:27,377 --> 00:03:28,487
Gather up.

80
00:03:31,147 --> 00:03:32,657
I felt a lot of things that day.

81
00:03:33,057 --> 00:03:35,627
We don't get that many chances to visit our parents...

82
00:03:35,727 --> 00:03:37,056
- at work. - Right.

83
00:03:37,057 --> 00:03:38,496
- That's right. - As for me,

84
00:03:38,497 --> 00:03:40,927
it was my first time seeing my father in action.

85
00:03:41,157 --> 00:03:42,597
I've gone and watched his games before.

86
00:03:42,927 --> 00:03:44,367
But when I saw him on the field,

87
00:03:44,867 --> 00:03:47,437
he wasn't the same guy I knew as my dad.

88
00:03:47,897 --> 00:03:48,966
- Really? - Yes.

89
00:03:48,967 --> 00:03:51,107
He was a lot more charismatic.

90
00:03:51,507 --> 00:03:53,577
Seeing a different side of my dad...

91
00:03:54,077 --> 00:03:56,507
made me very nervous that day.

92
00:03:56,747 --> 00:04:00,216
Was there another team you supported other than Yongin FC?

93
00:04:00,217 --> 00:04:03,486
No, I don't have a team I support in K League.

94
00:04:03,487 --> 00:04:05,757
His father's team is the team I support.

95
00:04:06,187 --> 00:04:08,286
- It's always been like that. - I saw you support FC Seoul.

96
00:04:08,287 --> 00:04:11,426
(I saw you support FC Seoul.)

97
00:04:11,427 --> 00:04:12,857
We went to the opening game together.

98
00:04:12,997 --> 00:04:15,327
I just like K League.

99
00:04:15,627 --> 00:04:16,996
But I don't support a specific team.

100
00:04:16,997 --> 00:04:19,196
I'm sure his father's team wasn't playing that day.

101
00:04:19,197 --> 00:04:20,236
Right. It was against Suwon.

102
00:04:20,237 --> 00:04:21,937
- Then... - I'd go and watch those games.

103
00:04:23,007 --> 00:04:24,637
- I got the wrong idea for a second. - I see.

104
00:04:24,967 --> 00:04:26,577
- You seem very flustered. - No, I'm not flustered.

105
00:04:27,007 --> 00:04:28,076
No.

106
00:04:28,077 --> 00:04:29,476
- I... - He got up from his seat.

107
00:04:29,477 --> 00:04:31,306
There's so much I want to say.

108
00:04:31,307 --> 00:04:33,677
He doesn't know much about this industry, so I'll keep quiet.

109
00:04:33,817 --> 00:04:35,047
- Okay. - Okay.

110
00:04:35,117 --> 00:04:37,087
You guys are going at each other today.

111
00:04:38,187 --> 00:04:40,317
I shouldn't let him get to me, but he's getting on my nerves.

112
00:04:40,487 --> 00:04:41,887
FC Seoul.

113
00:04:43,587 --> 00:04:44,787
MINHO, come over here.

114
00:04:45,297 --> 00:04:46,896
- He's sweet when he calls MINHO. - Come.

115
00:04:46,897 --> 00:04:47,897
I see. Okay.

116
00:04:47,898 --> 00:04:48,967
Hello.

117
00:04:49,297 --> 00:04:51,127
- Hello. - Hello.

118
00:04:51,297 --> 00:04:52,297
Hello.

119
00:04:53,197 --> 00:04:54,937
- Well... - Hello. I'm his son.

120
00:04:56,037 --> 00:04:57,067
Today,

121
00:04:58,007 --> 00:05:00,737
I'm just stopping by to watch you train.

122
00:05:01,137 --> 00:05:02,306
You guys are training really hard.

123
00:05:02,307 --> 00:05:04,077
I brought a coffee truck with me today.

124
00:05:04,377 --> 00:05:06,176
- Have some coffee on your way out. - That's good.

125
00:05:06,177 --> 00:05:07,246
- He seems proud. - Enjoy your dinner.

126
00:05:07,247 --> 00:05:08,646
- He's smiling. - Okay.

127
00:05:08,647 --> 00:05:10,316
- Great work, everyone. - Thank you.

128
00:05:10,317 --> 00:05:11,987
Good luck.

129
00:05:13,687 --> 00:05:15,986
(Turning back)

130
00:05:15,987 --> 00:05:18,086
Grab a cup of coffee on your way out.

131
00:05:18,087 --> 00:05:19,096
Okay.

132
00:05:19,097 --> 00:05:20,557
(Swarming out)

133
00:05:22,097 --> 00:05:23,997
- I can see he's feeling proud. - Right.

134
00:05:24,167 --> 00:05:25,796
He didn't know that I was bringing a coffee truck.

135
00:05:25,797 --> 00:05:26,866
- I see. - Okay.

136
00:05:26,867 --> 00:05:28,267
- It's nothing grand. - Okay.

137
00:05:28,667 --> 00:05:29,906
I brought a coffee truck...

138
00:05:29,907 --> 00:05:31,107
- and fruits. - That's...

139
00:05:31,637 --> 00:05:32,777
There it is.

140
00:05:33,377 --> 00:05:35,036
- He must feel so proud. - My gosh.

141
00:05:35,037 --> 00:05:36,046
(Let me see.)

142
00:05:36,047 --> 00:05:37,147
There it is.

143
00:05:37,707 --> 00:05:39,877
(Wishing Yongin FC a victory!)

144
00:05:40,477 --> 00:05:42,387
(MINHO booked a coffee truck as a surprise for his dad.)

145
00:05:42,947 --> 00:05:44,746
(He even prepared fruit boxes himself for the athletes.)

146
00:05:44,747 --> 00:05:45,786
That's a special gesture now.

147
00:05:45,787 --> 00:05:47,916
(Let's go, Choi Yun Kyum!)

148
00:05:47,917 --> 00:05:49,226
(Let's go, Choi Yun Kyum!)

149
00:05:49,227 --> 00:05:50,557
(From his son, MINHO)

150
00:05:50,727 --> 00:05:53,257
You should have put a picture of him making a heart with his fingers.

151
00:05:54,727 --> 00:05:55,727
Right.

152
00:05:55,997 --> 00:05:57,166
- But... - It's all letters.

153
00:05:57,167 --> 00:05:59,466
My father doesn't like that kind of stuff.

154
00:05:59,467 --> 00:06:00,796
You should still do it.

155
00:06:00,797 --> 00:06:02,006
If Hyun Moo sent the food truck,

156
00:06:02,007 --> 00:06:03,807
he would've put about 200 pictures of his face.

157
00:06:04,807 --> 00:06:06,906
It would've been right above, "It's a treat from Hyun Moo."

158
00:06:06,907 --> 00:06:08,176
- Right. - This is...

159
00:06:08,177 --> 00:06:09,807
I would've added a picture of me playing soccer.

160
00:06:10,547 --> 00:06:12,447
(The sender is the star, not the recipient.)

161
00:06:12,677 --> 00:06:14,416
How did you prepare all that?

162
00:06:14,417 --> 00:06:15,717
I did it quickly.

163
00:06:16,387 --> 00:06:17,586
- Do you... - Thank you.

164
00:06:17,587 --> 00:06:18,716
- Do you want a drink? - He's happy.

165
00:06:18,717 --> 00:06:19,756
- Do you... - Thank you.

166
00:06:19,757 --> 00:06:21,757
Do you want a drink?

167
00:06:22,057 --> 00:06:24,327
- I should. The truck is here. - Yes.

168
00:06:24,587 --> 00:06:27,027
Give me a non-caffeinated drink.

169
00:06:27,197 --> 00:06:28,497
- A non-caffeinated drink? - Thank you.

170
00:06:28,967 --> 00:06:31,297
Visit us with a coffee truck from time to time.

171
00:06:31,567 --> 00:06:32,666
- I... - Should it be a quarterly thing?

172
00:06:32,667 --> 00:06:35,207
I should make it a quarterly thing. I can come whenever you want.

173
00:06:36,437 --> 00:06:38,406
I didn't come because I didn't want to get in your way.

174
00:06:38,407 --> 00:06:39,707
(Smiling)

175
00:06:40,307 --> 00:06:41,737
You've never seen a coffee truck before, right?

176
00:06:42,407 --> 00:06:44,507
- Have you seen one? - It's my first time getting one.

177
00:06:45,947 --> 00:06:47,677
- I've never booked one either. - Right.

178
00:06:49,247 --> 00:06:50,916
I didn't know. But he was quite happy with it.

179
00:06:50,917 --> 00:06:53,116
- I bet he was so happy. - Of course, he'd be so happy.

180
00:06:53,117 --> 00:06:55,657
I had no idea. He jokingly said I should visit quarterly.

181
00:06:55,787 --> 00:06:58,126
- But if I did, he would be happy. - Of course.

182
00:06:58,127 --> 00:06:59,397
I felt it that day.

183
00:07:00,727 --> 00:07:02,597
- Hello. - Hello.

184
00:07:03,467 --> 00:07:04,896
- Get a drink. - Okay.

185
00:07:04,897 --> 00:07:06,136
(The volcano dad offers drinks from the coffee truck.)

186
00:07:06,137 --> 00:07:07,367
Have a drink before you go.

187
00:07:08,297 --> 00:07:09,567
Please have a drink, uncle.

188
00:07:10,037 --> 00:07:11,107
You're the best.

189
00:07:12,607 --> 00:07:14,706
Is this your drink, Dad? Is this the ade?

190
00:07:14,707 --> 00:07:15,977
Okay.

191
00:07:16,247 --> 00:07:18,617
(The son prepares the straw for his dad.)

192
00:07:19,017 --> 00:07:20,577
- You're preparing that for me. - Yes.

193
00:07:20,917 --> 00:07:22,187
- Thank you. - Enjoy your drink.

194
00:07:23,947 --> 00:07:26,117
(Sipping)

195
00:07:26,957 --> 00:07:28,186
(Approaching)

196
00:07:28,187 --> 00:07:29,256
Who's that?

197
00:07:29,257 --> 00:07:30,857
- Eul Yong's son. - Really?

198
00:07:30,957 --> 00:07:32,397
- Lee Eul Yong? - Seung Joon.

199
00:07:33,227 --> 00:07:35,396
- Nice to meet you. - Really?

200
00:07:35,397 --> 00:07:37,027
- His son is a soccer player too. - I see.

201
00:07:37,327 --> 00:07:38,966
We met when we were young. Do you remember?

202
00:07:38,967 --> 00:07:41,197
Do you not remember it at all? You were little.

203
00:07:41,697 --> 00:07:44,607
You were only a baby. You had just started walking.

204
00:07:45,007 --> 00:07:46,307
Don't get injured. Good luck.

205
00:07:47,077 --> 00:07:48,807
My father...

206
00:07:49,077 --> 00:07:52,577
trained Coach Lee Eul Yong.

207
00:07:53,117 --> 00:07:54,746
- Really? - Yes.

208
00:07:54,747 --> 00:07:56,087
Coach Lee Eul Yong's oldest son...

209
00:07:56,347 --> 00:07:58,317
- is a soccer player. - Yes.

210
00:07:58,347 --> 00:08:00,417
His second son is playing for Yongin FC.

211
00:08:00,717 --> 00:08:01,986
He's a promising player.

212
00:08:01,987 --> 00:08:03,356
- It's a family of soccer players. - Yes, it is.

213
00:08:03,357 --> 00:08:04,386
I see.

214
00:08:04,387 --> 00:08:05,797
You all know Lee Eul Yong, right?

215
00:08:06,127 --> 00:08:07,926
- You don't? - Did you watch the 2002 World Cup?

216
00:08:07,927 --> 00:08:09,126
- Lee Eul Yong... - Everyone knows him.

217
00:08:09,127 --> 00:08:10,127
- Lee Eul Yong. - You all watched it?

218
00:08:10,128 --> 00:08:11,397
- Do you not know him? - Of course, we know him.

219
00:08:12,197 --> 00:08:13,367
The famous Eul Yong smack.

220
00:08:13,667 --> 00:08:15,166
(The remedy for unsportsmanlike players)

221
00:08:15,167 --> 00:08:16,767
(The legendary Eul Yong smack)

222
00:08:17,967 --> 00:08:20,836
(Of course, I know him.)

223
00:08:20,837 --> 00:08:23,107
No, Dowoon. That's not it.

224
00:08:23,377 --> 00:08:25,447
- You have to turn around and do it. - Right.

225
00:08:25,677 --> 00:08:26,777
You must turn first.

226
00:08:27,017 --> 00:08:28,746
People only remember that when he pulled other great plays.

227
00:08:28,747 --> 00:08:29,987
(He had other powerful plays like the smack.)

228
00:08:30,487 --> 00:08:32,587
- Thank you. - Please enjoy.

229
00:08:32,857 --> 00:08:34,787
- Thank you. - Enjoy your drink.

230
00:08:35,527 --> 00:08:36,687
Good luck this week!

231
00:08:37,357 --> 00:08:38,757
- Thanks. - Sure. Enjoy.

232
00:08:39,697 --> 00:08:40,797
Enjoy.

233
00:08:41,727 --> 00:08:44,067
(Smiling thanks to his son)

234
00:08:44,697 --> 00:08:46,036
- Enjoy. - Thank you.

235
00:08:46,037 --> 00:08:47,437
- Thank you. - Thank you.

236
00:08:48,467 --> 00:08:50,566
I need to get changed. I'll change in the restroom.

237
00:08:50,567 --> 00:08:52,007
- Okay. See you in a bit. - Yes.

238
00:08:52,237 --> 00:08:53,406
- I'll be back. - Okay.

239
00:08:53,407 --> 00:08:54,507
Why are you getting changed?

240
00:08:54,977 --> 00:08:57,877
I always have workout clothes in my backpack.

241
00:08:58,817 --> 00:08:59,977
Not a surprise there.

242
00:09:00,447 --> 00:09:01,547
"Not a surprise there."

243
00:09:02,217 --> 00:09:03,286
Dad.

244
00:09:03,287 --> 00:09:06,887
(He's carrying his all-purpose bag as usual today.)

245
00:09:08,457 --> 00:09:09,956
This is why I brought my clothes.

246
00:09:09,957 --> 00:09:11,957
(When he goes into a restroom with his backpack,)

247
00:09:12,057 --> 00:09:13,057
(he can change into his workout clothes.)

248
00:09:13,058 --> 00:09:14,427
He's truly something else.

249
00:09:15,397 --> 00:09:17,897
Okay. Let's go.

250
00:09:19,967 --> 00:09:21,967
You have the legs of a soccer player.

251
00:09:24,167 --> 00:09:25,937
(As always, he's fully dressed in his workout clothes today.)

252
00:09:27,237 --> 00:09:29,307
- You fit right in on the field. - Your lower body...

253
00:09:29,647 --> 00:09:30,776
Okay. I'm ready!

254
00:09:30,777 --> 00:09:31,976
(My legs feel light today.)

255
00:09:31,977 --> 00:09:35,047
Your lower body isn't ideal for kicking balls.

256
00:09:35,217 --> 00:09:37,017
- What are you talking about? - You know?

257
00:09:37,147 --> 00:09:38,986
- I can kick any ball. - Only your cleats look good.

258
00:09:38,987 --> 00:09:41,157
- Only his cleats look good. - I can kick any ball.

259
00:09:41,417 --> 00:09:44,427
I came to show my support for my father.

260
00:09:44,887 --> 00:09:47,197
But I was on the soccer field, after all.

261
00:09:47,597 --> 00:09:49,967
I thought I ought to kick the ball a few times.

262
00:09:50,267 --> 00:09:52,797
I suggested that we play a casual game.

263
00:09:53,067 --> 00:09:54,137
A game?

264
00:09:54,397 --> 00:09:57,637
It was hitting the goalpost outside the penalty area.

265
00:09:58,167 --> 00:09:59,807
We had to only hit the top bar.

266
00:10:00,107 --> 00:10:01,536
Whoever hits it more will win.

267
00:10:01,537 --> 00:10:02,806
(Only the hits to the top bar will count.)

268
00:10:02,807 --> 00:10:04,446
- That's an original game. - It's not a shoot-out?

269
00:10:04,447 --> 00:10:05,576
They have to hit the goalpost?

270
00:10:05,577 --> 00:10:06,946
People play this game a lot more.

271
00:10:06,947 --> 00:10:08,417
It's a national game, almost.

272
00:10:08,547 --> 00:10:09,786
To increase accuracy?

273
00:10:09,787 --> 00:10:11,016
Every morning soccer club...

274
00:10:11,017 --> 00:10:12,286
- Okay. - plays this game.

275
00:10:12,287 --> 00:10:13,756
We play before we begin or at the end.

276
00:10:13,757 --> 00:10:16,526
We play this game to decide anything like the order and so on.

277
00:10:16,527 --> 00:10:17,657
Hitting the goalpost?

278
00:10:17,787 --> 00:10:19,826
Did your father teach you how to play soccer as a kid?

279
00:10:19,827 --> 00:10:22,527
- Never. Not even once. - That's interesting.

280
00:10:22,597 --> 00:10:23,866
I heard he didn't because he didn't want you...

281
00:10:23,867 --> 00:10:25,096
- to become a soccer player. - Right.

282
00:10:25,097 --> 00:10:26,697
- In case he became a soccer player? - Really?

283
00:10:26,867 --> 00:10:28,737
He strongly opposed the idea.

284
00:10:29,137 --> 00:10:31,337
- Not the idea of becoming an idol? - He wasn't against that.

285
00:10:32,777 --> 00:10:34,436
If he opposed that, MINHO might've become a soccer player.

286
00:10:34,437 --> 00:10:36,077
(Laughing)

287
00:10:36,347 --> 00:10:38,877
- It just couldn't be soccer. - Right, it couldn't be soccer.

288
00:10:39,017 --> 00:10:41,076
- The loser will pay for dinner. - Okay.

289
00:10:41,077 --> 00:10:43,117
His father used to play professionally too.

290
00:10:43,547 --> 00:10:45,556
- I bet he's competitive too. - Right.

291
00:10:45,557 --> 00:10:47,487
- He's as competitive as me. - The competitive father and son.

292
00:10:48,117 --> 00:10:49,426
- How many times will we play? - What?

293
00:10:49,427 --> 00:10:51,927
- How many times will we play? - There are four here.

294
00:10:52,327 --> 00:10:53,696
You must give me time to practice.

295
00:10:53,697 --> 00:10:54,726
You've been kicking the ball before I got here.

296
00:10:54,727 --> 00:10:56,726
- But I didn't. - I didn't either.

297
00:10:56,727 --> 00:10:59,537
- You kicked it here and there. - I only kicked it two times.

298
00:11:00,497 --> 00:11:02,136
(Their competitive side intensifies even before the game.)

299
00:11:02,137 --> 00:11:03,467
You can kick it twice then.

300
00:11:03,707 --> 00:11:05,837
- You can kick it twice then. - I'll do that to warm up a bit.

301
00:11:06,607 --> 00:11:08,536
- But you've been... - I'll just practice once.

302
00:11:08,537 --> 00:11:10,346
working out,

303
00:11:10,347 --> 00:11:11,946
- But... - so you have lower body strength.

304
00:11:11,947 --> 00:11:14,117
- But still. - I don't have lower body strength.

305
00:11:14,477 --> 00:11:15,987
I'm not a professional athlete.

306
00:11:16,747 --> 00:11:17,786
I'm not either.

307
00:11:17,787 --> 00:11:20,257
(I'm not either.)

308
00:11:20,987 --> 00:11:24,026
- I'll just practice one round. - Practice as much as you want.

309
00:11:24,027 --> 00:11:25,757
- I just need one round. That's it. - Okay.

310
00:11:26,797 --> 00:11:28,426
(Stretching)

311
00:11:28,427 --> 00:11:31,027
I can just kick it gently. I don't need to kick it hard, right?

312
00:11:31,327 --> 00:11:32,536
- Go ahead. - Okay.

313
00:11:32,537 --> 00:11:34,567
(He starts practicing amid subtle pressure.)

314
00:11:35,237 --> 00:11:36,307
My gosh.

315
00:11:36,737 --> 00:11:38,806
(The ball flies close to the goalpost on his first try.)

316
00:11:38,807 --> 00:11:39,877
He's good.

317
00:11:40,807 --> 00:11:41,807
(Okay. I got it.)

318
00:11:41,808 --> 00:11:43,647
- I got it. Okay. - Run and get the ball.

319
00:11:44,207 --> 00:11:45,307
- Run and get the ball. - Okay.

320
00:11:45,777 --> 00:11:47,617
(Running fast)

321
00:11:47,977 --> 00:11:49,117
(Laughing)

322
00:11:49,517 --> 00:11:51,187
He's too slow.

323
00:11:51,287 --> 00:11:52,917
(Unbelievable)

324
00:11:53,413 --> 00:11:55,213
Okay. Let's do it right away.

325
00:11:56,687 --> 00:11:59,056
(Flashy moves)

326
00:11:59,057 --> 00:12:00,957
It's been a while since we played together.

327
00:12:01,857 --> 00:12:03,496
(Not paying attention)

328
00:12:03,497 --> 00:12:04,697
Rock-paper-scissors to decide who goes first.

329
00:12:05,327 --> 00:12:06,596
I'll let you go first. Go ahead.

330
00:12:06,597 --> 00:12:08,736
- No. I want to go after you. - After me?

331
00:12:08,737 --> 00:12:09,967
- Yes. Okay. - I'll go first then.

332
00:12:10,407 --> 00:12:12,166
So you're really determined to beat your dad, you brat.

333
00:12:12,167 --> 00:12:14,907
(So you're really determined to beat your dad, you brat.)

334
00:12:15,237 --> 00:12:17,247
I won't go easy on you. You have to give it your all.

335
00:12:17,847 --> 00:12:20,377
- Of course. It's a match. - Right.

336
00:12:21,847 --> 00:12:23,987
He was on the national soccer team.

337
00:12:25,387 --> 00:12:28,787
(His dad is the oldest and the most experienced head coach in K League.)

338
00:12:29,687 --> 00:12:33,197
He's the source of my competitive side.

339
00:12:33,497 --> 00:12:35,767
I've gotten the competitive genes from him.

340
00:12:36,297 --> 00:12:39,897
- He used to play professionally. - I don't think he can beat his dad.

341
00:12:39,937 --> 00:12:42,136
- Right. - But you can't overlook age.

342
00:12:42,137 --> 00:12:43,607
- He's older now. - Right.

343
00:12:48,837 --> 00:12:51,107
(The volcano dad goes first.)

344
00:12:51,482 --> 00:12:52,992
Of course. It's a match.

345
00:12:53,492 --> 00:12:54,532
Right.

346
00:12:55,152 --> 00:12:58,892
(I'm still an active volcano.)

347
00:13:03,362 --> 00:13:04,862
- What? - He got it on his first try.

348
00:13:07,402 --> 00:13:09,402
(Falling flat)

349
00:13:10,042 --> 00:13:11,042
(Laughing)

350
00:13:11,642 --> 00:13:12,712
What are you doing?

351
00:13:13,342 --> 00:13:14,612
What was that, Dad?

352
00:13:15,912 --> 00:13:17,442
My aim is a bit off.

353
00:13:18,282 --> 00:13:19,951
What was that, Dad?

354
00:13:19,952 --> 00:13:21,212
But my ball went in.

355
00:13:21,982 --> 00:13:23,051
Okay. It's my turn.

356
00:13:23,052 --> 00:13:24,722
(The son is up next.)

357
00:13:25,122 --> 00:13:27,192
(Here's what you need to know about me.)

358
00:13:28,252 --> 00:13:31,091
(The living legend of "Idol Star Athletics Championships")

359
00:13:31,092 --> 00:13:32,391
He's the legend.

360
00:13:32,392 --> 00:13:33,632
(The living legend of "Idol Star Athletics Championships")

361
00:13:34,162 --> 00:13:35,261
(Do you think I'm going to lose?)

362
00:13:35,262 --> 00:13:37,062
(You think that's an option for me?)

363
00:13:37,302 --> 00:13:38,661
- It's over. - Okay. It's over.

364
00:13:38,662 --> 00:13:39,702
(Losing is never in the cards for me.)

365
00:13:40,632 --> 00:13:41,701
I think he can do it.

366
00:13:41,702 --> 00:13:43,702
(Getting ready for his fiery shot)

367
00:13:47,242 --> 00:13:50,611
(The ball is getting close to the top bar once again.)

368
00:13:50,612 --> 00:13:51,811
- He got it. - He did it.

369
00:13:51,812 --> 00:13:54,382
(Did he successfully gain the upper hand?)

370
00:14:00,252 --> 00:14:01,822
- Gosh. - Gosh, that was close.

371
00:14:03,222 --> 00:14:04,962
I think he can hit the top bar after a few tries.

372
00:14:05,162 --> 00:14:06,492
He got the hang of it, though.

373
00:14:06,732 --> 00:14:07,892
No, he's doomed.

374
00:14:08,632 --> 00:14:10,132
(Gosh, he's unbelievable.)

375
00:14:10,632 --> 00:14:12,201
- Gosh. - Gosh, that was close.

376
00:14:12,202 --> 00:14:13,202
(I almost had it.)

377
00:14:13,402 --> 00:14:15,402
(Relieved)

378
00:14:15,872 --> 00:14:17,641
(The father and the son both failed their 1st try.)

379
00:14:17,642 --> 00:14:19,342
It's your turn now. Go.

380
00:14:19,912 --> 00:14:20,972
Go.

381
00:14:21,172 --> 00:14:23,682
(I'm done with my warm-up.)

382
00:14:24,182 --> 00:14:25,411
(Zeroing in on the target)

383
00:14:25,412 --> 00:14:26,451
- He got it. - He got it.

384
00:14:26,452 --> 00:14:27,481
- He got it. - He really got it.

385
00:14:27,482 --> 00:14:28,482
He got it.

386
00:14:28,712 --> 00:14:29,752
That was crazy.

387
00:14:30,682 --> 00:14:32,952
- That was crazy. - His shots are a lot more accurate.

388
00:14:33,992 --> 00:14:36,162
(He zeroes in on the goalpost within two tries.)

389
00:14:39,632 --> 00:14:40,831
I was so surprised.

390
00:14:40,832 --> 00:14:42,961
Professionals can zero in much faster than us.

391
00:14:42,962 --> 00:14:44,701
Right. He got it on his second try.

392
00:14:44,702 --> 00:14:46,072
- That's amazing. - How is that possible?

393
00:14:46,372 --> 00:14:48,332
(This isn't good for me.)

394
00:14:48,832 --> 00:14:50,401
I've been playing soccer all my life.

395
00:14:50,402 --> 00:14:52,101
Hold on. That wasn't supposed to happen.

396
00:14:52,102 --> 00:14:53,172
(Flustered)

397
00:14:53,212 --> 00:14:54,441
- Okay. - Just hit it once.

398
00:14:54,442 --> 00:14:56,541
I think I'm going to beat you even if you do.

399
00:14:56,542 --> 00:14:58,542
No way. I'm going to make it happen now.

400
00:14:58,712 --> 00:14:59,751
I can feel it.

401
00:14:59,752 --> 00:15:01,282
I can't believe he hit the goalpost on his second try.

402
00:15:02,052 --> 00:15:04,482
(I'll catch up right away.)

403
00:15:05,282 --> 00:15:07,522
(Focusing all of his energy on his toes)

404
00:15:08,892 --> 00:15:10,392
Gosh, I kicked it too hard.

405
00:15:11,122 --> 00:15:12,532
- Gosh, I kicked it too hard. - Oh, my. What's he doing?

406
00:15:12,762 --> 00:15:14,332
I had too much energy that day.

407
00:15:15,262 --> 00:15:17,602
- Will you be able to sleep tonight? - No.

408
00:15:17,762 --> 00:15:19,932
- Because you lost to me... - No way.

409
00:15:21,902 --> 00:15:23,441
(Hitting 2 home runs in a row)

410
00:15:23,442 --> 00:15:24,872
(This is driving me crazy.)

411
00:15:25,342 --> 00:15:27,012
(You have a long way to go to catch up with me.)

412
00:15:27,212 --> 00:15:28,611
- The last one? - Yes, it's the last one.

413
00:15:28,612 --> 00:15:29,782
(It's already their fourth try.)

414
00:15:29,942 --> 00:15:32,482
- Either I win or it's a tie. - Right.

415
00:15:33,812 --> 00:15:35,412
- It's over if I hit the goalpost. - Okay.

416
00:15:37,052 --> 00:15:39,122
- Gosh. - What was that? Come on.

417
00:15:39,552 --> 00:15:42,691
(He wastes his last chance.)

418
00:15:42,692 --> 00:15:45,062
(Relieved)

419
00:15:45,592 --> 00:15:46,862
Let's take it to overtime.

420
00:15:47,662 --> 00:15:50,202
(I can't lose to him like this.)

421
00:15:51,202 --> 00:15:53,931
(Desperate)

422
00:15:53,932 --> 00:15:56,772
I need to relax more when I kick the ball.

423
00:15:57,672 --> 00:15:58,672
(Exhaling)

424
00:15:58,842 --> 00:16:00,112
It's a nerve-racking moment.

425
00:16:01,872 --> 00:16:03,481
(He's quite serious to beat his dad.)

426
00:16:03,482 --> 00:16:06,412
I was really serious about winning.

427
00:16:06,682 --> 00:16:08,111
You look like that when you eat tteokbokki.

428
00:16:08,112 --> 00:16:10,522
(This is nothing new to us.)

429
00:16:12,082 --> 00:16:14,121
(The last ball to decide their fate)

430
00:16:14,122 --> 00:16:17,562
(Here it goes!)

431
00:16:22,802 --> 00:16:24,401
Gosh, he's doomed.

432
00:16:24,402 --> 00:16:25,402
(Swooshing)

433
00:16:25,632 --> 00:16:26,972
(Sighing)

434
00:16:27,232 --> 00:16:30,672
- Let's go. Thanks for dinner. - No. Let's kick it one more time.

435
00:16:30,902 --> 00:16:32,712
We should kick at least 5 times. Let's kick it 1 more time.

436
00:16:32,912 --> 00:16:34,611
It's the last time. Let's kick it one more time.

437
00:16:34,612 --> 00:16:35,842
- He plays until he wins. - It's a shame to end it now.

438
00:16:37,142 --> 00:16:38,411
(Feeling guilty)

439
00:16:38,412 --> 00:16:40,581
What if he says the last one counts as two points?

440
00:16:40,582 --> 00:16:41,751
(He's about to insist the last shot counts as 2 points.)

441
00:16:41,752 --> 00:16:43,982
- Or 100 points. - You read my mind.

442
00:16:45,482 --> 00:16:46,752
It's a shame to end it now.

443
00:16:46,892 --> 00:16:48,421
You'll never hit the goalpost even if you have all day.

444
00:16:48,422 --> 00:16:51,022
(It's not a shame to end it here.)

445
00:16:52,492 --> 00:16:54,331
(The game is already over.)

446
00:16:54,332 --> 00:16:56,102
This is the last time. I promise.

447
00:16:57,362 --> 00:16:59,832
(Fine. I'll kick it one more time.)

448
00:17:01,802 --> 00:17:03,772
- Gosh. What's up with that? - Okay.

449
00:17:03,972 --> 00:17:05,972
- My aim is off too. - You must be nervous.

450
00:17:06,112 --> 00:17:07,242
I'll get it this time.

451
00:17:11,682 --> 00:17:13,552
- You're in my way. Move back. - What?

452
00:17:13,682 --> 00:17:15,451
- You're in my way. Move back. - What?

453
00:17:15,452 --> 00:17:16,752
Why did you stand in my way?

454
00:17:17,422 --> 00:17:19,352
(He's winning, but he still has to keep the pressure on.)

455
00:17:20,192 --> 00:17:21,892
It's my last chance. Okay.

456
00:17:22,192 --> 00:17:23,892
I must redeem myself with this chance.

457
00:17:28,662 --> 00:17:29,902
It's a match of pride,

458
00:17:30,232 --> 00:17:32,872
not between a father and a son. It's a battle, man to man.

459
00:17:33,902 --> 00:17:35,431
Your father must find you so cute.

460
00:17:35,432 --> 00:17:36,602
(His dad is at ease.)

461
00:17:36,702 --> 00:17:39,371
I'll aim for the top left corner.

462
00:17:39,372 --> 00:17:41,412
(It's time to show him my last fiery shot.)

463
00:17:43,982 --> 00:17:44,982
(Please!)

464
00:17:44,983 --> 00:17:46,152
- What? - No. He doesn't have it.

465
00:17:51,952 --> 00:17:53,222
- He got it. - He got it.

466
00:17:53,322 --> 00:17:55,791
(He ties the game with an extra turn.)

467
00:17:55,792 --> 00:17:56,792
(Giggling)

468
00:18:00,992 --> 00:18:02,932
(Screaming)

469
00:18:04,202 --> 00:18:05,502
He's that happy?

470
00:18:05,972 --> 00:18:07,031
(I said I wouldn't lose.)

471
00:18:07,032 --> 00:18:09,371
- Imagine if he missed it. - He'll lose his voice.

472
00:18:09,372 --> 00:18:10,872
(I said I wouldn't lose.)

473
00:18:12,812 --> 00:18:14,871
(Dreams come true.)

474
00:18:14,872 --> 00:18:16,282
No one is as competitive as him.

475
00:18:17,212 --> 00:18:19,812
(Is he that happy?)

476
00:18:19,912 --> 00:18:21,212
Bravo.

477
00:18:21,312 --> 00:18:23,021
(Dad, did you see that?)

478
00:18:23,022 --> 00:18:24,252
What's with the gaze?

479
00:18:24,352 --> 00:18:26,221
It's like he was doing a shoot-out at the World Cup.

480
00:18:26,222 --> 00:18:28,222
(It looks like he made the winning goal of the World Cup.)

481
00:18:28,352 --> 00:18:29,461
Let's go into overtime.

482
00:18:29,462 --> 00:18:31,822
- No, it was already over. - Let's take it to overtime.

483
00:18:31,962 --> 00:18:33,431
- You gave me a chance. - That's...

484
00:18:33,432 --> 00:18:36,232
- You can't insist that on me. - My gosh.

485
00:18:36,562 --> 00:18:39,032
- You shouldn't have let me do it. - We should do another game then.

486
00:18:39,532 --> 00:18:41,172
If both of us don't make the shot, it's over.

487
00:18:41,502 --> 00:18:43,142
- The last one? Okay. - Yes.

488
00:18:43,972 --> 00:18:46,042
(It's their final shot.)

489
00:18:46,742 --> 00:18:49,012
(Please fail.)

490
00:18:52,212 --> 00:18:54,452
(The trajectory looks good.)

491
00:18:55,712 --> 00:18:57,482
- That was so close. - My gosh. This is crazy.

492
00:18:58,752 --> 00:19:00,822
(Scared)

493
00:19:02,322 --> 00:19:04,062
I'm getting the hang of it now.

494
00:19:04,492 --> 00:19:05,522
As for me,

495
00:19:05,962 --> 00:19:07,661
I can hit the goalpost if I center my focus beforehand.

496
00:19:07,662 --> 00:19:08,732
- Okay. - Give me some time.

497
00:19:09,092 --> 00:19:10,131
Just gently.

498
00:19:10,132 --> 00:19:11,402
(Smiling)

499
00:19:12,302 --> 00:19:13,832
All right. Look at the spot.

500
00:19:14,732 --> 00:19:16,342
- Here it goes. - I don't think you can hit it.

501
00:19:17,502 --> 00:19:20,372
(No, you're wrong. I'm going to make the shot.)

502
00:19:24,482 --> 00:19:29,422
(Did centering his focus work?)

503
00:19:29,552 --> 00:19:31,181
(It was very effective!)

504
00:19:31,182 --> 00:19:32,182
Yes!

505
00:19:33,392 --> 00:19:35,752
(He beats his dad after relentlessly pestering him.)

506
00:19:35,952 --> 00:19:37,562
You must be so happy to beat your dad.

507
00:19:38,062 --> 00:19:40,232
(MINHO wins.)

508
00:19:40,592 --> 00:19:42,431
(Cheerful)

509
00:19:42,432 --> 00:19:44,462
Gosh, I beat him.

510
00:19:46,232 --> 00:19:48,332
- Fine, you won. - Good for you.

511
00:19:48,402 --> 00:19:50,371
- Fine, you won. - Good for you.

512
00:19:50,372 --> 00:19:51,572
You must be so happy to beat your dad.

513
00:19:51,902 --> 00:19:53,271
(Glancing over)

514
00:19:53,272 --> 00:19:55,872
- Congratulations. - Thank you.

515
00:19:56,412 --> 00:19:57,982
You're the best indeed.

516
00:19:58,382 --> 00:19:59,811
MINHO is the best.

517
00:19:59,812 --> 00:20:01,582
- He's unstoppable. - Yes.

518
00:20:01,882 --> 00:20:03,121
You're so cool.

519
00:20:03,122 --> 00:20:04,422
My gosh.

520
00:20:05,682 --> 00:20:06,752
Something feels off.

521
00:20:07,952 --> 00:20:10,092
It's been a while since he won without any cheers.

522
00:20:11,092 --> 00:20:13,461
- Before and after soccer games, - You...

523
00:20:13,462 --> 00:20:14,931
we used to make this bet from time to time.

524
00:20:14,932 --> 00:20:16,331
- It's the fairest bet. - Okay.

525
00:20:16,332 --> 00:20:18,202
I had never won before.

526
00:20:18,402 --> 00:20:20,931
He hasn't been training for two months.

527
00:20:20,932 --> 00:20:23,242
- No. This makes it clear. - When we played...

528
00:20:23,502 --> 00:20:25,571
- What do you mean? - He let you win.

529
00:20:25,572 --> 00:20:27,211
- He let you win. - Did he?

530
00:20:27,212 --> 00:20:29,272
You could never understand how deeply your father loves you.

531
00:20:32,182 --> 00:20:35,151
My gosh, I finally won. I always lost to him.

532
00:20:35,152 --> 00:20:36,352
(Clueless of his father's intention, the son is happy.)

533
00:20:37,682 --> 00:20:39,082
I finally won.

534
00:20:40,092 --> 00:20:41,352
I easily beat him.

535
00:20:42,752 --> 00:20:44,221
- You beat him? - Yes.

536
00:20:44,222 --> 00:20:45,722
Coach, you have to move the goalpost.

537
00:20:45,762 --> 00:20:47,462
The loser has to move the goalpost?

538
00:20:48,032 --> 00:20:50,601
The players are coming out because he lost.

539
00:20:50,602 --> 00:20:52,871
- Really? - Tell them to move the goalpost.

540
00:20:52,872 --> 00:20:54,332
Hey, the coach is going to move the goalpost.

541
00:20:54,402 --> 00:20:57,002
- Wait. - I beat your coach.

542
00:20:57,942 --> 00:21:00,371
I won, but I'll still move it.

543
00:21:00,372 --> 00:21:01,912
(Chuckling)

544
00:21:02,542 --> 00:21:04,942
(He moves the goalpost while taking all the credit.)

545
00:21:05,542 --> 00:21:08,482
(Cheering)

546
00:21:08,882 --> 00:21:10,722
My body suddenly feels light.

547
00:21:13,252 --> 00:21:15,221
The weather is great too. I'm having the best day.

548
00:21:15,222 --> 00:21:16,262
Okay.

549
00:21:17,222 --> 00:21:18,222
So you'll buy me dinner?

550
00:21:18,522 --> 00:21:20,292
You have to buy me dinner. You lost.

551
00:21:20,692 --> 00:21:22,161
There shouldn't have been a rematch.

552
00:21:22,162 --> 00:21:24,001
(They're bickering as they go to get changed.)

553
00:21:24,002 --> 00:21:25,162
I'm hungry.

554
00:21:27,172 --> 00:21:28,271
You're really off work now.

555
00:21:28,272 --> 00:21:30,372
- You walk out here to get off work. - Yes.

556
00:21:31,442 --> 00:21:33,571
- Thank you. - Thank you.

557
00:21:33,572 --> 00:21:35,972
Have a safe drive back. Thank you.

558
00:21:36,482 --> 00:21:38,281
It's still chilly at night.

559
00:21:38,382 --> 00:21:40,512
- Yes. - You have to bundle up.

560
00:21:42,582 --> 00:21:43,622
(Closing)

561
00:21:43,752 --> 00:21:45,222
- Let's make a pit stop... - Okay.

562
00:21:45,652 --> 00:21:47,822
- before we have dinner. - What?

563
00:21:47,892 --> 00:21:48,922
That was sudden.

564
00:21:49,192 --> 00:21:50,722
"Let's play Hit-the-Goalpost again."

565
00:21:54,292 --> 00:21:55,292
Let's go.

566
00:21:56,362 --> 00:21:58,762
(His dad is driving them somewhere.)

567
00:22:00,132 --> 00:22:01,802
Are you getting familiar with the neighborhood?

568
00:22:03,402 --> 00:22:04,741
- Not yet. - Not yet.

569
00:22:04,742 --> 00:22:05,872
- Right. - No.

570
00:22:17,052 --> 00:22:19,322
(Touching his nose)

571
00:22:20,752 --> 00:22:22,522
It's quiet when it's just you and your dad.

572
00:22:23,292 --> 00:22:25,421
And it was just the two of us in the car.

573
00:22:25,422 --> 00:22:28,061
I didn't know what to say to him.

574
00:22:28,062 --> 00:22:29,461
- Do you feel awkward around him? - Do you feel awkward?

575
00:22:29,462 --> 00:22:30,861
Strangely, I feel awkward in the car.

576
00:22:30,862 --> 00:22:32,261
- Really? - Yes, just in the car.

577
00:22:32,262 --> 00:22:33,501
- I don't get chatty in the car. - Right.

578
00:22:33,502 --> 00:22:35,301
- Do you talk a lot? - I...

579
00:22:35,302 --> 00:22:37,142
do talk a lot in the car.

580
00:22:37,472 --> 00:22:39,302
You're different although you're from Gyeongsang Province.

581
00:22:40,442 --> 00:22:42,772
- My dad is an egen-man. - An egen-man?

582
00:22:43,282 --> 00:22:44,611
"My dad is an egen-man."

583
00:22:44,612 --> 00:22:45,681
(Chattering)

584
00:22:45,682 --> 00:22:46,781
That was unexpected.

585
00:22:46,782 --> 00:22:48,212
I've never heard of a father being an egen-man.

586
00:22:48,682 --> 00:22:50,381
So he likes to chat. Is that it?

587
00:22:50,382 --> 00:22:52,682
- Yes, he does. - I see. It's different then.

588
00:22:54,492 --> 00:22:55,721
(MINHO's family is an average family.)

589
00:22:55,722 --> 00:22:57,092
It's the same with every family.

590
00:22:58,492 --> 00:23:00,962
(Looking around)

591
00:23:01,232 --> 00:23:03,931
What have you been doing for your exercise?

592
00:23:03,932 --> 00:23:05,032
- For my exercise? - Yes.

593
00:23:05,262 --> 00:23:06,702
- What do I do? - Yes.

594
00:23:06,902 --> 00:23:09,102
Well, I do a few activities.

595
00:23:10,302 --> 00:23:11,801
- A few activities? - Do you think I work out too much?

596
00:23:11,802 --> 00:23:13,172
Yes, you do.

597
00:23:13,912 --> 00:23:15,372
You won't do HYROX, right?

598
00:23:15,712 --> 00:23:16,811
No, I'm still doing it.

599
00:23:16,812 --> 00:23:17,941
When's the next contest? Is there another one?

600
00:23:17,942 --> 00:23:20,712
It was crazy how he won first place in the HYROX contest.

601
00:23:20,882 --> 00:23:23,081
- He won first place? - He did.

602
00:23:23,082 --> 00:23:24,121
He won first place?

603
00:23:24,122 --> 00:23:26,452
(A full-body fitness program he trained for)

604
00:23:26,592 --> 00:23:28,021
- That's amazing. - Gosh.

605
00:23:28,022 --> 00:23:29,022
I won at last.

606
00:23:29,023 --> 00:23:30,062
(Feeling proud)

607
00:23:30,222 --> 00:23:31,822
Gosh, that's incredible.

608
00:23:32,222 --> 00:23:33,432
(Glancing)

609
00:23:33,832 --> 00:23:35,992
- Let's do MOO-YROX. - MOO-YROX...

610
00:23:36,402 --> 00:23:37,631
Over my dead body.

611
00:23:37,632 --> 00:23:39,001
- I'll never do it... - Hyun Moo.

612
00:23:39,002 --> 00:23:40,071
no matter how trendy it is.

613
00:23:40,072 --> 00:23:42,531
- The age group... - No! I won't do it.

614
00:23:42,532 --> 00:23:43,601
- Hear me out. - Never.

615
00:23:43,602 --> 00:23:45,942
- You compete with your age group. - No.

616
00:23:46,172 --> 00:23:48,571
- You have a shot. - No.

617
00:23:48,572 --> 00:23:50,441
If you just complete the race, you could...

618
00:23:50,442 --> 00:23:51,811
- win third place. - I'll never be able to finish it.

619
00:23:51,812 --> 00:23:53,752
(I can never do it.)

620
00:23:54,312 --> 00:23:55,412
Where are we, Dad?

621
00:23:57,582 --> 00:23:59,452
I didn't know where we were going. I just followed him.

622
00:24:00,292 --> 00:24:02,192
- Is there a table? - Is it a cafe?

623
00:24:02,292 --> 00:24:03,661
- Hello. - Hello.

624
00:24:03,662 --> 00:24:06,462
(He follows his dad into a cafe.)

625
00:24:07,332 --> 00:24:09,392
Do you want me to get you coffee?

626
00:24:09,762 --> 00:24:11,362
- Yes. - What do you want? A latte?

627
00:24:11,832 --> 00:24:13,431
I'll drink a latte.

628
00:24:13,432 --> 00:24:14,502
- A latte? - Yes.

629
00:24:15,372 --> 00:24:16,872
- You'll buy me coffee? - Yes.

630
00:24:17,042 --> 00:24:18,701
Wait. I don't have my wallet.

631
00:24:18,702 --> 00:24:20,711
- Why don't you have a wallet? - It's in the car.

632
00:24:20,712 --> 00:24:22,241
- That's a classic move. - Yes.

633
00:24:22,242 --> 00:24:23,642
I didn't leave it behind on purpose.

634
00:24:24,812 --> 00:24:25,842
Here.

635
00:24:26,782 --> 00:24:27,852
Nice.

636
00:24:28,652 --> 00:24:29,652
(Flustered)

637
00:24:29,653 --> 00:24:30,982
I'm disappointed in MINHO.

638
00:24:32,322 --> 00:24:34,722
I see a lot of rising stars in the field of being a bad son.

639
00:24:36,122 --> 00:24:37,561
Why am I being a bad son?

640
00:24:37,562 --> 00:24:38,691
What's the point of winning first place in HYROX?

641
00:24:38,692 --> 00:24:39,832
You don't spend money on your parents.

642
00:24:41,192 --> 00:24:43,632
- He spends the most money on them. - I won.

643
00:24:47,302 --> 00:24:48,532
- Thank you. - Here you go.

644
00:24:48,572 --> 00:24:50,602
- You're serving the drinks. - Right.

645
00:24:51,972 --> 00:24:53,372
(Pulling the chair back)

646
00:24:53,512 --> 00:24:54,512
- Enjoy. - Okay.

647
00:24:54,512 --> 00:24:55,512
Thank you.

648
00:24:55,672 --> 00:24:57,182
Sit down.

649
00:24:57,842 --> 00:25:00,511
It's my signature latte with barley and coconut.

650
00:25:00,512 --> 00:25:01,911
- What is it? - Do they know each other?

651
00:25:01,912 --> 00:25:03,181
I think they know each other.

652
00:25:03,182 --> 00:25:04,221
(He joins their table.)

653
00:25:04,222 --> 00:25:05,882
Wait. What's going on?

654
00:25:06,082 --> 00:25:08,791
We've crossed paths when I was in Daejeon.

655
00:25:08,792 --> 00:25:09,962
- I see. - I see.

656
00:25:10,292 --> 00:25:13,931
He's the first sponsor of Yongin FC.

657
00:25:13,932 --> 00:25:15,361
(The 1st civilian to sponsor Yongin FC)

658
00:25:15,362 --> 00:25:17,302
Civic soccer clubs can receive...

659
00:25:17,432 --> 00:25:20,071
- sponsorship from local residents. - Right.

660
00:25:20,072 --> 00:25:21,102
- Right. - I see.

661
00:25:21,132 --> 00:25:23,142
He runs a dental clinic.

662
00:25:23,202 --> 00:25:26,341
To promote his dental clinic,

663
00:25:26,342 --> 00:25:27,841
he sponsors the club.

664
00:25:27,842 --> 00:25:28,972
(He's sponsoring the club to promote his clinic.)

665
00:25:29,272 --> 00:25:31,781
In a way, you're meeting your dad's fan.

666
00:25:31,782 --> 00:25:32,811
- Right. - Yes.

667
00:25:32,812 --> 00:25:34,011
His father's fan.

668
00:25:34,012 --> 00:25:35,982
I've heard a lot about you.

669
00:25:36,722 --> 00:25:39,222
- Thank you for coming. - Gosh, don't mention it.

670
00:25:39,682 --> 00:25:44,422
I wanted to introduce my son to you.

671
00:25:44,462 --> 00:25:45,621
His proud son.

672
00:25:45,622 --> 00:25:47,892
He comes to every match to root for our team.

673
00:25:48,192 --> 00:25:50,201
I'll come by and say hello next time.

674
00:25:50,202 --> 00:25:51,462
Thank you.

675
00:25:51,702 --> 00:25:53,772
So you've been rooting for my dad...

676
00:25:53,932 --> 00:25:55,902
- Oh, right. - ever since he was in Daejeon.

677
00:25:56,372 --> 00:25:58,342
- He must feel so grateful. - My gosh.

678
00:25:58,402 --> 00:26:01,772
I'm quite famous as a big soccer fan among my friends.

679
00:26:02,142 --> 00:26:03,911
I used to go and watch a lot of K League matches...

680
00:26:03,912 --> 00:26:05,111
when I was in Daejeon.

681
00:26:05,112 --> 00:26:08,552
He was appointed the head coach of the local team.

682
00:26:08,882 --> 00:26:12,522
I went to watch one of the games. And the rest is history.

683
00:26:13,252 --> 00:26:14,652
(Smiling)

684
00:26:14,792 --> 00:26:16,992
Back then, Daejeon...

685
00:26:17,462 --> 00:26:20,291
was known as the city of soccer.

686
00:26:20,292 --> 00:26:22,132
- It was no joke. - I can see his pride.

687
00:26:23,132 --> 00:26:26,432
(Back then, they were on a winning streak.)

688
00:26:26,902 --> 00:26:28,971
(They achieved the best performance in club history.)

689
00:26:28,972 --> 00:26:30,572
(Elated)

690
00:26:31,772 --> 00:26:34,872
I watched his career growing up.

691
00:26:35,242 --> 00:26:36,741
I always have this image...

692
00:26:36,742 --> 00:26:39,642
of what soccer should be in my head.

693
00:26:40,412 --> 00:26:42,051
- I remember the audience too. - He seems proud.

694
00:26:42,052 --> 00:26:43,982
- He's happy. - About 20,000 people showed up.

695
00:26:44,052 --> 00:26:46,122
You know the vibe from the old days.

696
00:26:46,422 --> 00:26:49,022
They were the good old days. I loved the stadium too.

697
00:26:49,152 --> 00:26:50,522
- I loved it. - Yes.

698
00:26:50,962 --> 00:26:52,521
Years have passed by.

699
00:26:52,522 --> 00:26:54,562
- And I set up a clinic in Yongin. - Yes.

700
00:26:54,832 --> 00:26:57,962
Then he was appointed the head coach of the club.

701
00:26:58,502 --> 00:27:00,201
- Seriously. What a precious bond. - They have a special bond.

702
00:27:00,202 --> 00:27:01,531
- That makes me happy. - I see.

703
00:27:01,532 --> 00:27:02,902
What an interesting history.

704
00:27:04,002 --> 00:27:06,741
Sometimes, I hear things like this. "I'm a fan of Coach Choi."

705
00:27:06,742 --> 00:27:08,612
"That's why I'm rooting for you too."

706
00:27:09,112 --> 00:27:11,811
I feel happier when I hear that.

707
00:27:11,812 --> 00:27:13,582
- That must make him feel good. - He must be happy.

708
00:27:13,652 --> 00:27:15,582
Meeting a fan of my father...

709
00:27:16,452 --> 00:27:19,252
Actually, I'm his son, but also his fan.

710
00:27:19,952 --> 00:27:23,292
So I was happy because it felt like I met a fellow fan.

711
00:27:23,492 --> 00:27:25,321
MINHO, can we take a picture together?

712
00:27:25,322 --> 00:27:27,061
- I'll take it before I leave. - Sure.

713
00:27:27,062 --> 00:27:28,932
- I'll take it then. - Thank you.

714
00:27:29,092 --> 00:27:30,402
Where should we take it?

715
00:27:30,632 --> 00:27:32,462
- The workers... - They're already setting it up.

716
00:27:33,972 --> 00:27:36,572
(He's as popular as his father.)

717
00:27:38,202 --> 00:27:40,041
(Feeling proud)

718
00:27:40,042 --> 00:27:41,672
I didn't know he looked that happy.

719
00:27:41,772 --> 00:27:42,911
- Thank you. - Thank you.

720
00:27:42,912 --> 00:27:44,742
- Let's get his autograph. - Sure.

721
00:27:45,782 --> 00:27:47,082
(Busy)

722
00:27:47,112 --> 00:27:49,012
I'm old news now that my son is here.

723
00:27:50,652 --> 00:27:52,522
- He must feel good about that too. - Right.

724
00:27:52,952 --> 00:27:54,152
He's proud.

725
00:27:54,722 --> 00:27:56,691
- Thank you for your hard work. - Thank you for your hard work.

726
00:27:56,692 --> 00:27:57,862
- Bye. - Bye.

727
00:27:58,162 --> 00:27:59,162
Okay.

728
00:27:59,822 --> 00:28:01,791
- They're so nice. - Where are they going next?

729
00:28:01,792 --> 00:28:02,932
(They head somewhere again.)

730
00:28:03,132 --> 00:28:05,032
They're totally in full bloom.

731
00:28:05,362 --> 00:28:06,402
Yes.

732
00:28:08,072 --> 00:28:09,532
(Peeking)

733
00:28:10,702 --> 00:28:14,772
(MINHO follows his dad as he leads the way.)

734
00:28:15,612 --> 00:28:17,682
(Looking around)

735
00:28:18,042 --> 00:28:20,111
It's different from a regular hotel.

736
00:28:20,112 --> 00:28:21,151
- Really? - Yes.

737
00:28:21,152 --> 00:28:22,711
- It feels different. - Right.

738
00:28:22,712 --> 00:28:23,752
Is it a dormitory?

739
00:28:24,282 --> 00:28:27,552
The club doesn't have a dormitory yet.

740
00:28:27,792 --> 00:28:29,592
They got him an apartment,

741
00:28:29,792 --> 00:28:31,562
so he currently lives there.

742
00:28:33,392 --> 00:28:34,892
- Welcome. - Okay.

743
00:28:36,832 --> 00:28:38,962
Seriously, there's nothing.

744
00:28:39,732 --> 00:28:42,102
There's nothing. What did you expect?

745
00:28:44,102 --> 00:28:45,141
- I mean... - Isn't it too empty?

746
00:28:45,142 --> 00:28:47,271
- It's too empty. - There's absolutely nothing.

747
00:28:47,272 --> 00:28:48,197
It's so clean.

748
00:28:48,572 --> 00:28:50,481
- I guess he only sleeps there. - He only sleeps there.

749
00:28:50,482 --> 00:28:52,582
(There's nothing more than meets the eye.)

750
00:28:53,652 --> 00:28:55,051
- It's totally empty. - Yes.

751
00:28:55,052 --> 00:28:56,252
(Looking around)

752
00:28:57,022 --> 00:28:59,051
(Is this it?)

753
00:28:59,052 --> 00:29:01,491
A washing machine is over here.

754
00:29:01,492 --> 00:29:02,492
Okay. I see.

755
00:29:02,493 --> 00:29:04,592
- There is a microwave too. - There are home appliances.

756
00:29:05,192 --> 00:29:08,032
There is a fridge. You can cook your meals here.

757
00:29:08,262 --> 00:29:09,262
That's right.

758
00:29:09,962 --> 00:29:13,901
(Let's open the fridge.)

759
00:29:13,902 --> 00:29:15,002
It must be empty.

760
00:29:15,432 --> 00:29:16,502
It's empty.

761
00:29:17,102 --> 00:29:18,501
- There's nothing. - Yes.

762
00:29:18,502 --> 00:29:21,672
(There's nothing.)

763
00:29:24,212 --> 00:29:26,952
You and Mom always make this with a milk carton.

764
00:29:27,612 --> 00:29:28,881
It's a container.

765
00:29:28,882 --> 00:29:30,622
- People did that back in the day. - It's convenient.

766
00:29:31,252 --> 00:29:32,481
- I mean... - Do you sleep on that bed?

767
00:29:32,482 --> 00:29:33,692
- What? - Where do you sleep?

768
00:29:33,852 --> 00:29:35,021
- Here. Yes. - Here?

769
00:29:35,022 --> 00:29:37,521
- Don't you use that bed? - I rarely use it.

770
00:29:37,522 --> 00:29:38,992
(He's curious about every little thing.)

771
00:29:41,562 --> 00:29:43,402
I'm just curious.

772
00:29:44,362 --> 00:29:45,762
Why would you be curious?

773
00:29:45,862 --> 00:29:47,832
I'm curious to know where you sleep.

774
00:29:48,872 --> 00:29:49,972
That's how it is.

775
00:29:50,972 --> 00:29:52,571
The situation has been reversed. When you enter a university,

776
00:29:52,572 --> 00:29:54,441
- your parents check out your place. - That's right.

777
00:29:54,442 --> 00:29:55,471
- Yes. - Your mom does that.

778
00:29:55,472 --> 00:29:57,042
- This is the same situation. - That's right.

779
00:29:57,342 --> 00:29:59,912
At what age did the family members start living separately?

780
00:30:00,052 --> 00:30:01,052
- Us? - Yes.

781
00:30:01,053 --> 00:30:03,212
Since my high school years. I was in 11th grade.

782
00:30:03,782 --> 00:30:07,221
I've lived away from home since then.

783
00:30:07,222 --> 00:30:08,691
- Exactly. - You've done the same.

784
00:30:08,692 --> 00:30:09,822
I've lived away from home too.

785
00:30:11,722 --> 00:30:13,532
- You brought that clock. - Yes.

786
00:30:14,392 --> 00:30:15,562
It was in your room.

787
00:30:15,862 --> 00:30:18,202
- He served in the Marine Corps. - I think it's MINHO's.

788
00:30:18,932 --> 00:30:20,202
I received it during my military service.

789
00:30:20,702 --> 00:30:22,702
It was a present that came with my certificate of merit.

790
00:30:23,672 --> 00:30:27,811
When I entered the Marine Corps,

791
00:30:27,812 --> 00:30:29,311
- my dad wasn't happy. - Okay.

792
00:30:29,312 --> 00:30:30,612
He was like, "Why take the difficult path?"

793
00:30:30,712 --> 00:30:32,982
But I think he's proud that I've served there.

794
00:30:33,182 --> 00:30:35,411
- He always carries that clock. - I see.

795
00:30:35,412 --> 00:30:36,981
(He always carries that clock.)

796
00:30:36,982 --> 00:30:38,822
- You use that clock very well. - Yes.

797
00:30:39,482 --> 00:30:41,192
- Is it convenient? - Yes.

798
00:30:41,492 --> 00:30:43,321
- The numbers are bright at night. - Okay.

799
00:30:43,322 --> 00:30:45,062
(I like that I can see the numbers easily at night.)

800
00:30:46,692 --> 00:30:48,561
(Shall I take a look inside the closet?)

801
00:30:48,562 --> 00:30:49,892
You don't have many clothes.

802
00:30:52,002 --> 00:30:53,532
Don't you need more clothes?

803
00:30:54,272 --> 00:30:57,002
- I only need my workout clothes... - That's right.

804
00:30:57,272 --> 00:30:58,902
- He leads a very simple life. - and a suit.

805
00:31:00,612 --> 00:31:03,112
(Let's see.)

806
00:31:03,612 --> 00:31:04,782
Didn't you wear this...

807
00:31:04,982 --> 00:31:06,812
- for the open match on a media day? - That's right.

808
00:31:06,982 --> 00:31:08,912
- Isn't it the suit we got together? - Yes.

809
00:31:09,182 --> 00:31:10,322
- I think so. - Right.

810
00:31:10,782 --> 00:31:12,981
- I'm surprised that you remember. - Of course. I wear it too.

811
00:31:12,982 --> 00:31:14,291
(Dad and two sons' matching looks)

812
00:31:14,292 --> 00:31:15,521
(MINHO's older brother, Min Seok)

813
00:31:15,522 --> 00:31:16,722
- That's cool. - How nice.

814
00:31:17,422 --> 00:31:19,721
I think we should get another one. Why are you against the idea?

815
00:31:19,722 --> 00:31:21,192
- We don't need another one. - What?

816
00:31:21,932 --> 00:31:23,601
Why do you and Mom always say no...

817
00:31:23,602 --> 00:31:26,132
when I want to do something for you guys?

818
00:31:28,432 --> 00:31:30,271
- We don't want your help. - My goodness.

819
00:31:30,272 --> 00:31:31,641
(Chuckling)

820
00:31:31,642 --> 00:31:34,672
Why not? It's just a suit.

821
00:31:35,372 --> 00:31:38,042
- I have two suits in the office. - Okay.

822
00:31:38,142 --> 00:31:39,642
I have one in the closet.

823
00:31:39,742 --> 00:31:42,282
I find that very fascinating.

824
00:31:42,712 --> 00:31:44,981
My mom asks me to buy her things, but my dad doesn't.

825
00:31:44,982 --> 00:31:47,422
That's good. You should be happy when your parents ask for things.

826
00:31:47,622 --> 00:31:48,622
I'm serious.

827
00:31:48,623 --> 00:31:50,491
Dowoon, your dad must ask for things since he's an egen-guy.

828
00:31:50,492 --> 00:31:51,962
Yes, he does.

829
00:31:53,222 --> 00:31:56,391
My parents go, "We need a new bed."

830
00:31:56,392 --> 00:31:57,532
That's so nice.

831
00:31:57,732 --> 00:31:59,432
It's nice indeed.

832
00:31:59,632 --> 00:32:01,172
It puts my mind at ease.

833
00:32:01,272 --> 00:32:03,072
- That's right. - Of course, it does.

834
00:32:03,172 --> 00:32:05,802
My mom keeps trying to stash cash.

835
00:32:06,042 --> 00:32:07,941
- "Stash?" - "Stash?"

836
00:32:07,942 --> 00:32:10,011
How could you say that, you punk?

837
00:32:10,012 --> 00:32:12,481
Don't say that about your mom. Seriously.

838
00:32:12,482 --> 00:32:13,851
She doesn't need anything.

839
00:32:13,852 --> 00:32:15,651
At every occasion,

840
00:32:15,652 --> 00:32:17,082
- she asks for cash. - Cash?

841
00:32:17,182 --> 00:32:18,882
- That's understandable. - Cash is the best.

842
00:32:19,082 --> 00:32:21,921
That's why you need to keep saying...

843
00:32:21,922 --> 00:32:23,622
that you're struggling financially.

844
00:32:24,792 --> 00:32:27,162
- That came out of nowhere. - My goodness.

845
00:32:27,492 --> 00:32:29,591
- Hold on. Suddenly... - "I don't have any money."

846
00:32:29,592 --> 00:32:30,761
- Keep saying that. - Why...

847
00:32:30,762 --> 00:32:32,761
- Okay. - Why did this take a strange turn?

848
00:32:32,762 --> 00:32:34,671
- Hold on. - Don't call me unfilial.

849
00:32:34,672 --> 00:32:36,201
- You know... - I became so startled just now.

850
00:32:36,202 --> 00:32:37,642
- I can't believe these people. - Seriously.

851
00:32:38,202 --> 00:32:39,842
So this is how people feel when they look at me.

852
00:32:39,942 --> 00:32:41,911
- You know... - I became so startled.

853
00:32:41,912 --> 00:32:43,612
It won't be easy to win this match.

854
00:32:43,712 --> 00:32:46,142
- I'll need to write down this tip. - Even I haven't thought of it.

855
00:32:47,812 --> 00:32:49,051
(I want to buy my dad another suit.)

856
00:32:49,052 --> 00:32:50,182
- He feels uncomfortable. - Yes.

857
00:32:50,882 --> 00:32:53,322
- You only sleep here. - Yes.

858
00:32:55,392 --> 00:32:57,522
My dad kept going, "There's nothing."

859
00:32:57,792 --> 00:33:00,232
- "Why would you visit?" - Dads are always like that.

860
00:33:00,362 --> 00:33:02,892
When I visited the apartment, there really was nothing.

861
00:33:03,532 --> 00:33:04,832
It made me feel more...

862
00:33:06,462 --> 00:33:07,532
It was a bit...

863
00:33:09,002 --> 00:33:10,132
I felt for him, to be honest.

864
00:33:10,742 --> 00:33:13,102
He must be bored and lonely there.

865
00:33:13,772 --> 00:33:18,082
He always gets stressed out in the world of competition.

866
00:33:18,412 --> 00:33:19,612
There's a lot on his mind.

867
00:33:20,812 --> 00:33:22,382
When I think about...

868
00:33:23,112 --> 00:33:25,152
how he spends his time in that empty apartment...

869
00:33:26,082 --> 00:33:27,122
It was...

870
00:33:27,652 --> 00:33:31,222
one of the moments that made me feel a bit apologetic.

871
00:33:31,892 --> 00:33:33,832
"Living here alone must feel lonely."

872
00:33:34,592 --> 00:33:38,002
"I'll need to call him often." I had that thought.

873
00:33:39,262 --> 00:33:43,171
I wanted to visit and see how you were living.

874
00:33:43,172 --> 00:33:44,172
I see.

875
00:33:44,442 --> 00:33:45,941
- Let's go. Let's head out. - Okay.

876
00:33:45,942 --> 00:33:47,472
(I'll visit next time.)

877
00:33:47,772 --> 00:33:49,372
We should fill the fridge.

878
00:33:50,072 --> 00:33:51,282
We don't need to do that.

879
00:33:53,142 --> 00:33:54,882
- Let's make a quick stop. - Okay.

880
00:33:57,422 --> 00:33:59,452
- They'll buy something. - MINHO will buy him something.

881
00:33:59,652 --> 00:34:01,122
- Hello. - Welcome.

882
00:34:01,622 --> 00:34:04,862
I'm looking for sunscreen. Where are the sunscreen sticks?

883
00:34:05,092 --> 00:34:06,721
If you're looking for one for men,

884
00:34:06,722 --> 00:34:08,191
- this is a good choice. - Okay.

885
00:34:08,192 --> 00:34:10,631
- Is it for your dad? - This one is the best.

886
00:34:10,632 --> 00:34:11,662
Okay.

887
00:34:11,932 --> 00:34:14,202
Dad, you never put on sunscreen.

888
00:34:14,402 --> 00:34:15,501
- You... - Is that why we're here?

889
00:34:15,502 --> 00:34:16,801
Yes, that's why we're here.

890
00:34:16,802 --> 00:34:17,802
My goodness.

891
00:34:17,803 --> 00:34:18,842
(Dad, I'm here to buy you sunscreen.)

892
00:34:19,772 --> 00:34:22,611
(For his dad who works under the scorching sun,)

893
00:34:22,612 --> 00:34:25,842
(he wants to give a present.)

894
00:34:27,682 --> 00:34:28,812
It's TAEMIN.

895
00:34:29,412 --> 00:34:30,522
- Is it TAEMIN? - Yes.

896
00:34:31,152 --> 00:34:33,622
(It's good to see him.)

897
00:34:34,092 --> 00:34:35,392
Let's buy TAEMIN's.

898
00:34:36,122 --> 00:34:37,191
- Shall we do that? - Yes.

899
00:34:37,192 --> 00:34:38,392
He's your bandmate.

900
00:34:38,892 --> 00:34:41,532
This one might not suit your skin.

901
00:34:42,232 --> 00:34:43,262
Let's buy two.

902
00:34:43,592 --> 00:34:44,902
- Two are enough. - Okay.

903
00:34:45,532 --> 00:34:46,971
What else do you need? Do you have lotion?

904
00:34:46,972 --> 00:34:48,232
Yes, I do. It's okay.

905
00:34:48,702 --> 00:34:50,701
- Let's buy it while we're here. - No.

906
00:34:50,702 --> 00:34:51,702
- Are you sure? - Yes.

907
00:34:51,802 --> 00:34:53,101
- How about a cleansing foam? - I have it.

908
00:34:53,102 --> 00:34:54,172
- Do you have it? - Yes.

909
00:34:54,942 --> 00:34:56,982
- Do you have lotion? - I have it.

910
00:34:57,182 --> 00:34:58,281
- He has everything. - He says he has everything.

911
00:34:58,282 --> 00:35:00,141
- Hello. - Are you ready to pay?

912
00:35:00,142 --> 00:35:01,852
- You wanted to come here for that. - Yes.

913
00:35:02,852 --> 00:35:04,522
- Please insert your card. - Okay.

914
00:35:04,952 --> 00:35:06,281
- Thank you. Have a good night. - Thank you.

915
00:35:06,282 --> 00:35:07,892
- Thank you. - Thank you.

916
00:35:08,322 --> 00:35:09,952
- Let's go and eat now. - Okay.

917
00:35:10,252 --> 00:35:11,462
Thank you. I'll use them well.

918
00:35:11,892 --> 00:35:13,561
I realized something that day.

919
00:35:13,562 --> 00:35:15,731
- I just need to do it. - You just need to buy it.

920
00:35:15,732 --> 00:35:17,701
- He doesn't tell you what he needs. - No.

921
00:35:17,702 --> 00:35:19,561
You should visit again and check.

922
00:35:19,562 --> 00:35:21,232
The sun stick might be brand new.

923
00:35:21,572 --> 00:35:23,472
Visit when there's a match and put it on his face.

924
00:35:23,702 --> 00:35:25,172
Do it at the stadium.

925
00:35:25,602 --> 00:35:26,671
- Shall I go in advance? - Yes.

926
00:35:26,672 --> 00:35:28,642
Run to Yongin. That's a good idea.

927
00:35:29,742 --> 00:35:31,012
I'm really hungry now.

928
00:35:33,012 --> 00:35:34,912
- This place is clean and tasty. - I see.

929
00:35:35,982 --> 00:35:37,821
- Hello. - Welcome.

930
00:35:37,822 --> 00:35:39,252
- Is it a gukbap place? - Hello.

931
00:35:39,682 --> 00:35:41,052
Is it a barbecue place?

932
00:35:41,322 --> 00:35:42,822
It seems like a rib place.

933
00:35:43,122 --> 00:35:44,162
Hello.

934
00:35:45,492 --> 00:35:46,861
- Here you go. - Thank you.

935
00:35:46,862 --> 00:35:48,032
(Food gets prepared right away.)

936
00:35:49,092 --> 00:35:50,732
- Marinated crabs. - Marinated crabs?

937
00:35:50,962 --> 00:35:52,032
This place is popular.

938
00:35:52,832 --> 00:35:54,032
The side dishes look nice.

939
00:35:55,502 --> 00:35:57,442
(Glancing over)

940
00:35:58,002 --> 00:35:59,402
Did the owner step outside?

941
00:35:59,502 --> 00:36:01,072
We want to say hello properly.

942
00:36:02,442 --> 00:36:03,782
Sir, please come here for a second.

943
00:36:04,042 --> 00:36:05,142
Let's say hello properly.

944
00:36:06,042 --> 00:36:07,051
Hello.

945
00:36:07,052 --> 00:36:09,351
- He knows this owner. - He knows this owner too.

946
00:36:09,352 --> 00:36:11,582
- He knows all the owners. - I was born in 1990.

947
00:36:11,782 --> 00:36:13,251
- You're a year older than me. - A year older.

948
00:36:13,252 --> 00:36:14,891
- I look ten years older. - No.

949
00:36:14,892 --> 00:36:17,361
You did ssireum back in the day.

950
00:36:17,362 --> 00:36:18,522
- That's right. - Really?

951
00:36:18,722 --> 00:36:20,192
I should've known from your arms...

952
00:36:20,592 --> 00:36:23,292
- and body. - It looks like you've lost weight.

953
00:36:23,462 --> 00:36:25,331
I'm trying to lose weight before my wedding.

954
00:36:25,332 --> 00:36:26,802
- Really? Congratulations. - Congratulations.

955
00:36:26,932 --> 00:36:28,271
- Thank you. - We'll enjoy the food.

956
00:36:28,272 --> 00:36:29,871
- I'll prepare your food quickly. - Okay.

957
00:36:29,872 --> 00:36:31,072
(He brags about his son at every restaurant he frequents.)

958
00:36:32,742 --> 00:36:34,441
It looks like high-quality meat.

959
00:36:34,442 --> 00:36:36,571
- My goodness. - It looks so delicious.

960
00:36:36,572 --> 00:36:37,681
It looks tasty.

961
00:36:37,682 --> 00:36:39,141
- It looks tasty. - It's well-marbled.

962
00:36:39,142 --> 00:36:40,412
(Dinner with high-quality meat)

963
00:36:40,852 --> 00:36:42,152
I should pay for the meal.

964
00:36:42,712 --> 00:36:44,682
- I still make money. - Okay.

965
00:36:45,722 --> 00:36:46,952
A bet is a bet.

966
00:36:47,392 --> 00:36:49,122
- Didn't you bring your wallet? - He'll pay, right?

967
00:36:49,322 --> 00:36:51,091
I'm sure he'll pay this time.

968
00:36:51,092 --> 00:36:52,121
No way.

969
00:36:52,122 --> 00:36:55,762
I won the game of hitting the goalpost, so...

970
00:36:56,262 --> 00:36:58,162
You're reaping the rewards until late at night.

971
00:36:59,062 --> 00:37:01,672
I've bought him many meals.

972
00:37:01,832 --> 00:37:03,101
He must've gone there to eat for free.

973
00:37:03,102 --> 00:37:05,872
(Fiery unfilial son)

974
00:37:08,072 --> 00:37:09,872
(Putting)

975
00:37:10,272 --> 00:37:12,111
I'll grill the meat.

976
00:37:12,112 --> 00:37:13,381
You should do that much for a free meal.

977
00:37:13,382 --> 00:37:14,582
Grill it nicely.

978
00:37:14,912 --> 00:37:16,212
You're paying for it.

979
00:37:19,182 --> 00:37:20,182
It looks tasty.

980
00:37:20,183 --> 00:37:21,921
(Beef gets grilled nicely.)

981
00:37:21,922 --> 00:37:23,152
That's the tastiest.

982
00:37:24,092 --> 00:37:26,422
(Gulping)

983
00:37:27,432 --> 00:37:28,991
(Excuse me.)

984
00:37:28,992 --> 00:37:30,962
- Thank you. - This is on the house.

985
00:37:31,102 --> 00:37:32,562
- Is it cheonggukjang? - Yes.

986
00:37:32,702 --> 00:37:33,732
Nice.

987
00:37:33,872 --> 00:37:35,071
You like cheonggukjang.

988
00:37:35,072 --> 00:37:36,601
- It's the version you like. - Yes.

989
00:37:36,602 --> 00:37:37,802
It has many chilis.

990
00:37:39,342 --> 00:37:41,171
(He fills a bowl with it.)

991
00:37:41,172 --> 00:37:43,311
He and I like the same food.

992
00:37:43,312 --> 00:37:44,412
- I see. - All right.

993
00:37:45,542 --> 00:37:47,042
- Thank you. - Okay.

994
00:37:48,412 --> 00:37:50,312
Try the marinated crabs. You like the dish.

995
00:37:50,982 --> 00:37:51,982
Eat them first.

996
00:37:53,552 --> 00:37:54,651
(He tries his favorite dish, cheonggukjang, first.)

997
00:37:54,652 --> 00:37:57,891
You and your dad know each other's taste.

998
00:37:57,892 --> 00:37:59,862
I find that touching.

999
00:38:00,462 --> 00:38:02,732
It's nice to eat cheonggukjang for the first time in a while.

1000
00:38:04,062 --> 00:38:06,161
(The dad and son drink the soup in the same manner.)

1001
00:38:06,162 --> 00:38:07,232
I love the soup.

1002
00:38:09,532 --> 00:38:11,372
(Picking up a piece of meat)

1003
00:38:11,672 --> 00:38:13,002
- Eat up. - Okay.

1004
00:38:14,072 --> 00:38:16,612
(Dad, please eat up.)

1005
00:38:17,542 --> 00:38:18,881
(He eats the meat his son has grilled.)

1006
00:38:18,882 --> 00:38:19,941
Eat up.

1007
00:38:19,942 --> 00:38:21,812
(Nodding)

1008
00:38:24,922 --> 00:38:25,922
(Impressed)

1009
00:38:26,482 --> 00:38:27,722
- Isn't it tasty? - It's tasty.

1010
00:38:31,792 --> 00:38:32,921
(The meat tastes even better because his dad is paying.)

1011
00:38:32,922 --> 00:38:34,491
The meat was so tasty.

1012
00:38:34,492 --> 00:38:35,632
(The meat tastes even better because his dad is paying.)

1013
00:38:36,092 --> 00:38:37,161
- It's delicious. - Yes.

1014
00:38:37,162 --> 00:38:38,162
(He's eating with gusto.)

1015
00:38:38,532 --> 00:38:39,832
- Try this as well. - Okay.

1016
00:38:41,802 --> 00:38:43,472
(He eats mushroom too.)

1017
00:38:44,302 --> 00:38:47,142
Dad, you always said this when I was young.

1018
00:38:48,042 --> 00:38:49,512
- When we were in the elevator, - Okay.

1019
00:38:49,872 --> 00:38:52,111
you always said that you weren't my dad,

1020
00:38:52,112 --> 00:38:53,341
but a middle-aged man from the neighboring house.

1021
00:38:53,342 --> 00:38:54,712
- Yes. - You always said that.

1022
00:38:55,212 --> 00:38:57,221
- I got fooled as a child. - Why?

1023
00:38:57,222 --> 00:38:59,082
You always said that you got me from under the bridge.

1024
00:38:59,822 --> 00:39:01,192
I cried about it.

1025
00:39:01,752 --> 00:39:05,591
When I was young, I didn't think that I resembled you a lot.

1026
00:39:05,592 --> 00:39:06,962
I thought I resembled Mom.

1027
00:39:08,292 --> 00:39:10,362
MINHO takes after his mom a lot.

1028
00:39:11,262 --> 00:39:12,401
How cute!

1029
00:39:12,402 --> 00:39:13,531
(When he was young, MINHO took after his mom a lot.)

1030
00:39:13,532 --> 00:39:15,902
Now that I'm older, I definitely resemble you.

1031
00:39:16,902 --> 00:39:18,102
In what ways?

1032
00:39:18,202 --> 00:39:20,341
- I have your jawline... - Okay.

1033
00:39:20,342 --> 00:39:21,472
and eyes.

1034
00:39:22,342 --> 00:39:26,481
From the back, you guys walk the same.

1035
00:39:26,482 --> 00:39:28,182
- Seriously. - You guys walk the same.

1036
00:39:28,782 --> 00:39:30,852
I'm glad that you only resemble the good things.

1037
00:39:31,282 --> 00:39:32,782
Do you think I have your competitiveness?

1038
00:39:33,482 --> 00:39:34,822
I think you have my competitiveness.

1039
00:39:35,222 --> 00:39:37,362
I also hate losing.

1040
00:39:37,892 --> 00:39:39,322
But while I don't show it,

1041
00:39:39,822 --> 00:39:42,962
you show it with your actions and facial expressions.

1042
00:39:43,632 --> 00:39:44,902
With your actions and facial expressions.

1043
00:39:45,462 --> 00:39:47,472
(His competitiveness explodes.)

1044
00:39:47,632 --> 00:39:51,141
When you lose a match, it shows on your face.

1045
00:39:51,142 --> 00:39:52,372
(Upset)

1046
00:39:53,142 --> 00:39:54,711
- But I'm worse. - Yes.

1047
00:39:54,712 --> 00:39:55,742
(I admit it.)

1048
00:39:55,842 --> 00:39:57,012
(Screaming)

1049
00:39:57,642 --> 00:39:59,142
I don't know why I make it so obvious.

1050
00:40:00,512 --> 00:40:02,812
(Staring)

1051
00:40:03,912 --> 00:40:05,222
You exercise...

1052
00:40:05,982 --> 00:40:09,521
and influence people in a good way.

1053
00:40:09,522 --> 00:40:11,091
(Despite your competitiveness, you're a good influence.)

1054
00:40:11,092 --> 00:40:13,062
I think you're going in a nice direction.

1055
00:40:14,232 --> 00:40:16,532
I'm not really good at saying such things to people.

1056
00:40:17,532 --> 00:40:18,962
I don't do that.

1057
00:40:19,202 --> 00:40:21,502
Still, I'm grateful for you.

1058
00:40:21,972 --> 00:40:23,331
I'm proud of you.

1059
00:40:23,332 --> 00:40:25,142
(You grew up well.)

1060
00:40:25,802 --> 00:40:28,411
I'm grateful that you grew up well.

1061
00:40:28,412 --> 00:40:31,011
In a sense, you've become...

1062
00:40:31,012 --> 00:40:32,941
the pride of the family.

1063
00:40:32,942 --> 00:40:35,782
- All of your aunts... - It looks like he's a bit moved.

1064
00:40:36,412 --> 00:40:37,922
- love you. - Really?

1065
00:40:38,722 --> 00:40:40,252
You're my son.

1066
00:40:40,552 --> 00:40:43,221
"He grew up well. You raised him well."

1067
00:40:43,222 --> 00:40:46,362
I feel the happiest when I hear such words.

1068
00:40:47,332 --> 00:40:48,862
(MINHO, come.)

1069
00:40:49,032 --> 00:40:50,062
He's proud of his son.

1070
00:40:50,162 --> 00:40:53,802
(He wants to introduce his son who is his pride to everyone.)

1071
00:40:54,132 --> 00:40:55,432
You raised me well.

1072
00:40:56,872 --> 00:41:00,471
To be honest, he rarely says such words.

1073
00:41:00,472 --> 00:41:03,642
I think he was happy that day.

1074
00:41:03,842 --> 00:41:06,042
His son came to cheer him on.

1075
00:41:06,842 --> 00:41:08,511
It's the same for you.

1076
00:41:08,512 --> 00:41:12,351
Many people tell me that you're the coach...

1077
00:41:12,352 --> 00:41:13,482
they respect the most.

1078
00:41:13,852 --> 00:41:15,292
Many dads tell me...

1079
00:41:15,922 --> 00:41:17,821
that you're the best dad.

1080
00:41:17,822 --> 00:41:19,892
I hear that very often.

1081
00:41:20,122 --> 00:41:22,391
Those words make me happy.

1082
00:41:22,392 --> 00:41:24,132
"My dad is cool."

1083
00:41:24,362 --> 00:41:25,601
"He isn't all words."

1084
00:41:25,602 --> 00:41:27,331
"He shows with his actions."

1085
00:41:27,332 --> 00:41:28,602
I feel that often.

1086
00:41:30,132 --> 00:41:32,242
- Don't you need to eat more? - No, I'm full.

1087
00:41:32,702 --> 00:41:33,972
I ate everything, Dad.

1088
00:41:34,142 --> 00:41:35,342
Don't be ridiculous.

1089
00:41:35,572 --> 00:41:37,381
I ate a lot today for the first time in a while.

1090
00:41:37,382 --> 00:41:38,382
(Clean)

1091
00:41:38,612 --> 00:41:40,312
Does Mom know that I had dinner with you?

1092
00:41:40,612 --> 00:41:41,752
Of course, she does.

1093
00:41:42,112 --> 00:41:43,712
- Shall we call her? - Yes.

1094
00:41:44,522 --> 00:41:45,682
She must be curious.

1095
00:41:48,852 --> 00:41:50,392
- MINHO. - Mom.

1096
00:41:50,792 --> 00:41:51,862
What are you doing?

1097
00:41:52,122 --> 00:41:53,762
- Nothing. - Nothing?

1098
00:41:54,062 --> 00:41:55,231
Did you have dinner with your dad?

1099
00:41:55,232 --> 00:41:57,032
Yes, we had beef.

1100
00:41:57,292 --> 00:42:00,502
Well done. Your dad's face became gaunt.

1101
00:42:01,472 --> 00:42:03,032
He lost so much weight.

1102
00:42:03,132 --> 00:42:05,372
It's because he has many worries.

1103
00:42:05,642 --> 00:42:06,941
- Mom. - Yes?

1104
00:42:06,942 --> 00:42:08,012
He needs to overcome it.

1105
00:42:11,282 --> 00:42:13,911
He should learn how to overcome it from you.

1106
00:42:13,912 --> 00:42:15,282
Dad needs to overcome it.

1107
00:42:15,782 --> 00:42:17,522
I came here to give him strength today.

1108
00:42:18,282 --> 00:42:19,352
Well done.

1109
00:42:20,382 --> 00:42:23,792
To be honest, I felt very happy...

1110
00:42:24,722 --> 00:42:25,922
throughout the day.

1111
00:42:26,622 --> 00:42:29,162
But at the same time, I felt a bit sad.

1112
00:42:30,492 --> 00:42:32,962
I thought he'd kick the ball with ease,

1113
00:42:33,862 --> 00:42:37,372
but he wasn't how he used to be.

1114
00:42:39,202 --> 00:42:41,542
I realized that many years have passed.

1115
00:42:42,112 --> 00:42:44,912
Also, we feel happy...

1116
00:42:45,382 --> 00:42:46,612
when we're together.

1117
00:42:47,042 --> 00:42:49,182
I felt bad that I hadn't visited sooner.

1118
00:42:50,082 --> 00:42:51,652
Whenever people ask me...

1119
00:42:52,382 --> 00:42:54,052
what kind of a person I want to be,

1120
00:42:54,422 --> 00:42:56,122
I always say that I want to be...

1121
00:42:56,222 --> 00:42:57,592
someone like my dad.

1122
00:42:58,362 --> 00:43:00,361
If I'm doing well,

1123
00:43:00,362 --> 00:43:02,162
he praises me continuously.

1124
00:43:02,732 --> 00:43:04,732
If I'm clumsy or lacking in any way,

1125
00:43:05,132 --> 00:43:08,132
he scolds me as his dad.

1126
00:43:08,672 --> 00:43:11,672
It's thanks to him that I've grown well.

1127
00:43:13,842 --> 00:43:17,782
To this day, I want to be...

1128
00:43:18,212 --> 00:43:20,542
my dad's biggest fan...

1129
00:43:21,012 --> 00:43:22,952
and dependable son.

1130
00:43:25,352 --> 00:43:27,052
- I enjoyed the food. - Okay.

1131
00:43:27,252 --> 00:43:28,892
- It was really nice. - I'm glad you enjoyed it.

1132
00:43:29,492 --> 00:43:32,062
I got this dinner since I won fairly.

1133
00:43:33,222 --> 00:43:35,292
That isn't true.

1134
00:43:36,392 --> 00:43:38,531
- Thank you for the food. Let's go. - Okay.

1135
00:43:38,532 --> 00:43:40,662
(The Everlasting Fire That Shines Upon Me)

1136
00:43:40,958 --> 00:43:42,358
I suddenly had this thought...

1137
00:43:42,498 --> 00:43:44,698
for the first time ever.

1138
00:43:45,172 --> 00:43:47,172
I want a son like him.

1139
00:43:47,712 --> 00:43:48,771
Same here.

1140
00:43:48,772 --> 00:43:50,372
- Right? - Yes. It won't be half bad.

1141
00:43:50,981 --> 00:43:53,681
Even though I'm not even thinking about marriage,

1142
00:43:54,282 --> 00:43:57,482
I thought about how nice it'd be to have a son like him.

1143
00:43:58,382 --> 00:44:00,052
You never know.

1144
00:44:00,322 --> 00:44:02,422
He might cause troubles when we aren't watching.

1145
00:44:03,192 --> 00:44:04,792
- Right? We only saw the good side. - Yes.

1146
00:44:04,962 --> 00:44:06,291
You only saw the good side.

1147
00:44:06,292 --> 00:44:07,532
Okay. Never mind then.

1148
00:44:08,062 --> 00:44:09,132
I changed my mind.

1149
00:44:09,632 --> 00:44:10,762
(I Live Alone)

1150
00:44:16,832 --> 00:44:19,372
(Spring Comes Early)

1151
00:44:21,412 --> 00:44:27,612
(Si Hyeon, Little Tiger, is deep asleep.)

1152
00:44:35,122 --> 00:44:38,622
(Alarm wakes her up.)

1153
00:44:40,362 --> 00:44:42,162
(Stretching)

1154
00:44:46,602 --> 00:44:48,102
Will she go fishing at 5am?

1155
00:44:48,372 --> 00:44:49,432
Fishing?

1156
00:44:50,172 --> 00:44:51,742
You only see through the lens of your interest.

1157
00:44:52,442 --> 00:44:54,202
Golfers will say, "Will she have a round of golf?"

1158
00:44:54,512 --> 00:44:56,612
That's when I think about going to bed.

1159
00:44:56,842 --> 00:44:58,942
It's the perfect time for meeting Mr. Ito Junji.

1160
00:45:03,752 --> 00:45:04,425
It isn't her home.

1161
00:45:05,225 --> 00:45:06,825
The bed looks different.

1162
00:45:07,052 --> 00:45:10,752
You're right. That's the lodging I go to all the time.

1163
00:45:11,562 --> 00:45:12,761
That looks amazing.

1164
00:45:12,762 --> 00:45:14,192
(Where is she?)

1165
00:45:14,692 --> 00:45:16,661
This is Tongyeong.

1166
00:45:16,662 --> 00:45:18,661
Tongyeong. It's a nice place.

1167
00:45:18,662 --> 00:45:19,762
Tongyeong is amazing.

1168
00:45:21,132 --> 00:45:22,232
Tongyeong is incredible.

1169
00:45:23,132 --> 00:45:24,402
What's famous in Tongyeong?

1170
00:45:24,902 --> 00:45:26,872
Tongyeong is famous for gimbap and oysters.

1171
00:45:27,342 --> 00:45:29,712
The Battle of Hansan Island.

1172
00:45:29,842 --> 00:45:30,981
That's right.

1173
00:45:30,982 --> 00:45:32,211
- Did that happen in Tongyeong? - Yes.

1174
00:45:32,212 --> 00:45:34,381
- There are statues... - That's right.

1175
00:45:34,382 --> 00:45:35,611
- and the Turtle Ship. - That's right.

1176
00:45:35,612 --> 00:45:36,751
That's why their gimbap is called Chungmu gimbap.

1177
00:45:36,752 --> 00:45:39,451
- Yi Sun Sin's title, Chungmugong. - Is that where it gets the name?

1178
00:45:39,452 --> 00:45:40,521
Really?

1179
00:45:40,522 --> 00:45:42,091
- There's also honey bread. - That's right.

1180
00:45:42,092 --> 00:45:43,351
- Right? Honey bread. - Yes.

1181
00:45:43,352 --> 00:45:45,261
Ji Hyeon knows everything about the south.

1182
00:45:45,262 --> 00:45:46,391
I know everything.

1183
00:45:46,392 --> 00:45:48,061
(He's from Mokpo.)

1184
00:45:48,062 --> 00:45:49,092
(Proud)

1185
00:45:50,892 --> 00:45:51,961
It's so sunny.

1186
00:45:51,962 --> 00:45:53,102
(Compared to Seoul, Tongyeong is 5°C warmer.)

1187
00:45:53,332 --> 00:45:55,101
Since it's in the south,

1188
00:45:55,102 --> 00:45:58,772
it welcomes spring two weeks earlier than Seoul.

1189
00:45:59,672 --> 00:46:01,242
I'm here to enjoy early spring.

1190
00:46:03,212 --> 00:46:05,512
(Let's hurry up and head out.)

1191
00:46:06,282 --> 00:46:07,311
(The current time is 5:14am.)

1192
00:46:07,312 --> 00:46:08,612
It's so early.

1193
00:46:09,752 --> 00:46:12,581
(Walking fast)

1194
00:46:12,582 --> 00:46:13,681
Where is she going?

1195
00:46:13,682 --> 00:46:14,692
(Walking fast)

1196
00:46:19,362 --> 00:46:20,462
The sun is rising.

1197
00:46:22,462 --> 00:46:26,532
(She begins to walk faster.)

1198
00:46:28,202 --> 00:46:29,631
I can almost smell early morning from the video.

1199
00:46:29,632 --> 00:46:30,802
(She arrives at place after walking fast.)

1200
00:46:32,272 --> 00:46:33,372
It's so nice.

1201
00:46:34,202 --> 00:46:36,212
I've arrived at Seoho Market.

1202
00:46:37,272 --> 00:46:39,581
(A place with fresh agricultural and marine products)

1203
00:46:39,582 --> 00:46:40,982
Does it open so early in the morning?

1204
00:46:41,552 --> 00:46:44,522
Right next to the market is where they auction off...

1205
00:46:45,022 --> 00:46:46,181
seafood.

1206
00:46:46,182 --> 00:46:48,251
It begins at 4am.

1207
00:46:48,252 --> 00:46:50,022
Since this market is close...

1208
00:46:50,192 --> 00:46:52,091
- Seafood comes straight here. - Yes.

1209
00:46:52,092 --> 00:46:53,192
(Seaweed from the auction can be seen here and there.)

1210
00:46:53,692 --> 00:46:56,762
Seoho Market has produces...

1211
00:46:56,892 --> 00:46:59,302
grandmas have dug up in the countryside.

1212
00:46:59,475 --> 00:47:01,315
You need to visit early in the morning...

1213
00:47:01,458 --> 00:47:03,057
to get good ingredients...

1214
00:47:03,172 --> 00:47:05,472
before middle-aged women do.

1215
00:47:05,942 --> 00:47:07,141
The sellers bring...

1216
00:47:07,142 --> 00:47:09,241
small amount of precious ingredients...

1217
00:47:09,242 --> 00:47:10,912
you can't get in Seoul.

1218
00:47:11,412 --> 00:47:14,781
At Gyeongdong Market, you can buy...

1219
00:47:14,782 --> 00:47:17,452
top-quality produces.

1220
00:47:17,822 --> 00:47:20,852
At Seoho Market, you can buy precious ingredients...

1221
00:47:21,092 --> 00:47:23,052
- that won't go to Seoul. Yes. - Rare items.

1222
00:47:23,122 --> 00:47:24,692
You can buy such ingredients.

1223
00:47:26,362 --> 00:47:28,862
Be careful with your position as the greens expert.

1224
00:47:29,162 --> 00:47:31,632
There's someone who wants to take your position.

1225
00:47:33,632 --> 00:47:34,972
It's Bae In Hyuk.

1226
00:47:35,102 --> 00:47:37,301
- Exactly. I watched the episode. - He wants to take your spot.

1227
00:47:37,302 --> 00:47:38,372
- Did you watch it? - Yes.

1228
00:47:38,702 --> 00:47:41,811
(He established himself as the shepherd's purse expert...)

1229
00:47:41,812 --> 00:47:43,941
(and threatened Si Hyeon's position.)

1230
00:47:43,942 --> 00:47:45,481
- He recognized shepherd's purse. - I watched it.

1231
00:47:45,482 --> 00:47:46,982
- Can you do that? - Of course.

1232
00:47:49,482 --> 00:47:50,781
So there can be a competition with vegetables.

1233
00:47:50,782 --> 00:47:52,222
Of course.

1234
00:47:52,282 --> 00:47:54,052
I don't want to lose when it comes to vegetables.

1235
00:47:56,692 --> 00:47:57,791
Hello.

1236
00:47:57,792 --> 00:47:58,862
(The Little Tiger hunts for rare vegetables again today.)

1237
00:47:58,922 --> 00:48:00,191
Where are you from?

1238
00:48:00,192 --> 00:48:01,832
- I'm from Seoul. - Pardon?

1239
00:48:01,862 --> 00:48:03,562
- I'm from Seoul. - I see.

1240
00:48:05,862 --> 00:48:09,202
I'll get five dollars worth of this.

1241
00:48:09,402 --> 00:48:10,731
It's 3 dollars for 1.

1242
00:48:10,732 --> 00:48:11,971
It's 3 dollars for 1?

1243
00:48:11,972 --> 00:48:13,641
- I'll just get 1 portion then. - Just 1?

1244
00:48:13,642 --> 00:48:15,112
- Yes. - It's wild-caught.

1245
00:48:15,242 --> 00:48:18,382
The greens of the sea are the sea vegetables.

1246
00:48:18,542 --> 00:48:19,881
The seaweed is literally a wet vegetable.

1247
00:48:19,882 --> 00:48:20,982
- Right. - Greens of the sea.

1248
00:48:21,582 --> 00:48:22,712
- Thank you. - Bye.

1249
00:48:24,552 --> 00:48:26,521
How much is it for this much sweet shrimp?

1250
00:48:26,522 --> 00:48:28,292
- It's 20 dollars. - It's 20 dollars?

1251
00:48:28,392 --> 00:48:29,992
They're really expensive in Seoul.

1252
00:48:30,522 --> 00:48:32,921
Can I get this one?

1253
00:48:32,922 --> 00:48:33,922
- This one? - Yes.

1254
00:48:33,923 --> 00:48:34,961
- They're delicious. - They're so sweet.

1255
00:48:34,962 --> 00:48:36,062
They're really good.

1256
00:48:36,832 --> 00:48:38,632
Gosh, they look so good.

1257
00:48:39,462 --> 00:48:40,732
Hello.

1258
00:48:41,272 --> 00:48:43,401
- Hello. - You haven't gone back yet.

1259
00:48:43,402 --> 00:48:46,341
Right. I came last week. I came back again this week.

1260
00:48:46,342 --> 00:48:48,512
- Gosh. - I came yesterday.

1261
00:48:48,942 --> 00:48:50,912
It isn't easy to visit Tongyeong often.

1262
00:48:51,382 --> 00:48:52,811
Is there a reason you visit Tongyeong often?

1263
00:48:52,812 --> 00:48:53,841
There are many other places.

1264
00:48:53,842 --> 00:48:55,411
As you know, Tongyeong has...

1265
00:48:55,412 --> 00:48:57,952
- a lot of great seafood. - Right.

1266
00:48:58,122 --> 00:48:59,351
Including the salted seafood.

1267
00:48:59,352 --> 00:49:01,452
- Right. - Mokpo has great seafood as well.

1268
00:49:01,652 --> 00:49:04,352
Mokpo is great as well, but...

1269
00:49:04,692 --> 00:49:07,561
But the products that are produced are definitely different.

1270
00:49:07,562 --> 00:49:08,691
They're definitely different.

1271
00:49:08,692 --> 00:49:09,891
The Yellow Sea is known for its mud flat.

1272
00:49:09,892 --> 00:49:11,062
- Right. - Right.

1273
00:49:11,502 --> 00:49:12,702
Hello.

1274
00:49:13,002 --> 00:49:14,202
Welcome.

1275
00:49:15,902 --> 00:49:17,031
It's my first time seeing this.

1276
00:49:17,032 --> 00:49:18,501
- The hairy crab. - The hairy crab.

1277
00:49:18,502 --> 00:49:19,601
(She spots the hairy crab this time.)

1278
00:49:19,602 --> 00:49:20,671
You must have them when you visit Tongyeong.

1279
00:49:20,672 --> 00:49:21,741
Right.

1280
00:49:21,742 --> 00:49:23,872
- Is that so? - But they're hard to find.

1281
00:49:23,972 --> 00:49:25,541
- They're hard to find. - They aren't sold all the time.

1282
00:49:25,542 --> 00:49:27,112
They must be bought whenever you see them.

1283
00:49:27,382 --> 00:49:29,151
How do hairy crabs taste different?

1284
00:49:29,152 --> 00:49:31,881
For me, the meat felt really soft.

1285
00:49:31,882 --> 00:49:32,951
Right. The meat is really sweet as well.

1286
00:49:32,952 --> 00:49:34,021
Right.

1287
00:49:34,022 --> 00:49:35,992
I think I've only tried them marinated.

1288
00:49:36,222 --> 00:49:37,651
Hairy crabs are quite small.

1289
00:49:37,652 --> 00:49:39,191
- Right. - It's hard to get their meat.

1290
00:49:39,192 --> 00:49:40,761
That is why they are often marinated.

1291
00:49:40,762 --> 00:49:42,461
The innards taste so good.

1292
00:49:42,462 --> 00:49:43,462
- Right. - Right.

1293
00:49:43,463 --> 00:49:44,802
The savory taste is enough to kill.

1294
00:49:45,002 --> 00:49:47,332
I've tried hairy crabs in Japan.

1295
00:49:47,402 --> 00:49:49,732
But there was something I realized for the first time there.

1296
00:49:50,032 --> 00:49:52,741
Koreans are the only ones to mix rice with the innards.

1297
00:49:52,742 --> 00:49:54,042
- Really? - Really?

1298
00:49:54,342 --> 00:49:56,311
To distinguish Koreans in Japan,

1299
00:49:56,312 --> 00:49:59,211
just look for people who are mixing rice with innards.

1300
00:49:59,212 --> 00:50:00,741
Koreans are the only ones to do that.

1301
00:50:00,742 --> 00:50:01,952
That's a must when eating crabs.

1302
00:50:02,812 --> 00:50:04,212
Awesome.

1303
00:50:05,122 --> 00:50:07,281
- I'll take all of them. - Yes?

1304
00:50:07,282 --> 00:50:08,452
- Would you like all of them? - Yes.

1305
00:50:09,222 --> 00:50:11,092
- Gosh. - She's buying a lot.

1306
00:50:11,792 --> 00:50:14,062
That and the sea cucumbers.

1307
00:50:14,692 --> 00:50:15,732
Sea cucumbers.

1308
00:50:16,562 --> 00:50:17,962
I'll get the clam meat as well.

1309
00:50:18,962 --> 00:50:20,931
- I'll get some oysters as well. - Oysters?

1310
00:50:20,932 --> 00:50:22,032
(She spends more on seafood.)

1311
00:50:23,172 --> 00:50:24,671
The big ones would be great.

1312
00:50:24,672 --> 00:50:26,102
(Cute)

1313
00:50:28,272 --> 00:50:30,641
The sargassum is nowhere to be seen.

1314
00:50:30,642 --> 00:50:31,642
- Sorry? - The sargassum.

1315
00:50:31,643 --> 00:50:32,711
The sargassum?

1316
00:50:32,712 --> 00:50:34,912
- I wanted to get some sargassum. - What is the sargassum?

1317
00:50:35,412 --> 00:50:36,911
The sargassum is a sea vegetable as well.

1318
00:50:36,912 --> 00:50:39,251
It has these beads.

1319
00:50:39,252 --> 00:50:40,981
It's a sea vegetable that looks very pretty.

1320
00:50:40,982 --> 00:50:44,092
I saw them the week before, but I couldn't find them this week.

1321
00:50:44,452 --> 00:50:45,662
It was really disappointing.

1322
00:50:46,992 --> 00:50:48,862
Awesome. They look delicious.

1323
00:50:50,532 --> 00:50:53,001
(She hunts a box of different kinds of seafood.)

1324
00:50:53,002 --> 00:50:54,462
- Here you are. - Right.

1325
00:50:54,772 --> 00:50:56,572
- Thank you. - Goodbye.

1326
00:50:57,132 --> 00:50:59,542
She looks like someone who is doing a chore.

1327
00:50:59,942 --> 00:51:01,002
Hello.

1328
00:51:02,612 --> 00:51:05,582
(She heads somewhere with both hands full.)

1329
00:51:06,212 --> 00:51:07,412
Where is she going?

1330
00:51:08,052 --> 00:51:09,411
Nice. There's no one else.

1331
00:51:09,412 --> 00:51:11,681
(She arrives at a restaurant?)

1332
00:51:11,682 --> 00:51:12,782
Gosh!

1333
00:51:13,982 --> 00:51:15,492
- Hello. - Hello.

1334
00:51:17,292 --> 00:51:20,892
(She puts down her things on one side naturally.)

1335
00:51:22,132 --> 00:51:23,161
Would you like the puffer fish soup?

1336
00:51:23,162 --> 00:51:24,292
- Yes. - Right.

1337
00:51:26,402 --> 00:51:27,732
In Tongyeong,

1338
00:51:28,002 --> 00:51:31,542
there are famous dishes which I have for breakfast.

1339
00:51:31,842 --> 00:51:34,672
It's the sirak soup and the puffer fish soup.

1340
00:51:34,842 --> 00:51:36,842
Whenever I visited the market,

1341
00:51:36,972 --> 00:51:39,882
I went to this restaurant to have puffer fish soup.

1342
00:51:40,712 --> 00:51:42,212
The puffer fish soup must be famous.

1343
00:51:42,482 --> 00:51:44,611
The puffer fish soup is my favorite food.

1344
00:51:44,612 --> 00:51:45,721
- The puffer fish soup. - Right.

1345
00:51:45,722 --> 00:51:47,151
The dried radish greens soup is called the sirak soup.

1346
00:51:47,152 --> 00:51:48,821
Right. That's what it's called in their dialect.

1347
00:51:48,822 --> 00:51:50,292
- Sirak soup. - The dried radish greens soup.

1348
00:51:50,322 --> 00:51:52,121
In the market,

1349
00:51:52,122 --> 00:51:53,762
there's a separate alley for sirak soup.

1350
00:51:54,192 --> 00:51:56,061
The ppaettaegi porridge is sold there as well.

1351
00:51:56,062 --> 00:51:57,161
- Ppaettaegi porridge? - Ppaettaegi porridge?

1352
00:51:57,162 --> 00:51:59,932
The ppaettaegi porridge is made of sweet potato.

1353
00:52:00,362 --> 00:52:01,602
- Dried sweet potato. - Right.

1354
00:52:01,902 --> 00:52:03,671
I can sense a bit of dialect from you.

1355
00:52:03,672 --> 00:52:05,401
- Right. - Right.

1356
00:52:05,402 --> 00:52:06,402
Where are you from?

1357
00:52:06,602 --> 00:52:08,071
(Interested)

1358
00:52:08,072 --> 00:52:09,211
Where are you from?

1359
00:52:09,212 --> 00:52:11,911
My parents are from Gyeongsang Province.

1360
00:52:11,912 --> 00:52:13,211
- I see. - My mom is from Busan...

1361
00:52:13,212 --> 00:52:14,812
and my dad is from Gyeongju.

1362
00:52:15,052 --> 00:52:17,052
- Then you're one of us. - I see.

1363
00:52:18,622 --> 00:52:19,751
They look so happy.

1364
00:52:19,752 --> 00:52:20,752
(The ones who speak in a dialect look happy.)

1365
00:52:20,852 --> 00:52:22,521
You two should become close friends.

1366
00:52:22,522 --> 00:52:24,221
- Us? - Gosh.

1367
00:52:24,222 --> 00:52:26,192
- We're close. - Really?

1368
00:52:26,462 --> 00:52:27,921
- We're really close. - Right.

1369
00:52:27,922 --> 00:52:29,391
The Yeongnam region and Honam region have united.

1370
00:52:29,392 --> 00:52:30,431
Right. That's great to see.

1371
00:52:30,432 --> 00:52:31,961
It's the Hwagae Market with the two of them together.

1372
00:52:31,962 --> 00:52:33,501
- Hwagae Market? - Hwagae Market?

1373
00:52:33,502 --> 00:52:34,502
Right.

1374
00:52:34,503 --> 00:52:36,231
- I got his autograph. - The regions have united.

1375
00:52:36,232 --> 00:52:38,041
The two of them could be called Hwagae Market.

1376
00:52:38,042 --> 00:52:40,141
- I love it. Hwagae Market. - Right.

1377
00:52:40,142 --> 00:52:41,941
Right. You two should release an album as Hwagae Market.

1378
00:52:41,942 --> 00:52:43,012
- Right. - What?

1379
00:52:43,342 --> 00:52:44,342
Let's do it!

1380
00:52:44,782 --> 00:52:45,841
Right!

1381
00:52:45,842 --> 00:52:48,652
(Please show your support for the new group, Hwagae Market.)

1382
00:52:48,912 --> 00:52:50,812
(Getting out)

1383
00:52:51,282 --> 00:52:52,882
(Pulling)

1384
00:52:54,052 --> 00:52:56,021
(She ties her hair first.)

1385
00:52:56,022 --> 00:52:57,292
It's her attitude toward food.

1386
00:52:59,162 --> 00:53:00,821
(After getting ready to welcome the food...)

1387
00:53:00,822 --> 00:53:02,631
- Your food is here. - Thank you.

1388
00:53:02,632 --> 00:53:03,661
Awesome!

1389
00:53:03,662 --> 00:53:04,692
(The food gets served quickly.)

1390
00:53:06,332 --> 00:53:08,172
- There are so many side dishes. - It's amazing, isn't it?

1391
00:53:09,002 --> 00:53:10,431
Why is the anchovy so big?

1392
00:53:10,432 --> 00:53:11,802
Tongyeong's Gijang anchovy.

1393
00:53:12,202 --> 00:53:13,342
Gijang anchovies are big.

1394
00:53:14,202 --> 00:53:15,711
The raw silver pomfret is served as a side dish.

1395
00:53:15,712 --> 00:53:16,872
(Even the raw silver pomfret gets served.)

1396
00:53:17,842 --> 00:53:19,241
(The deeply boiled puffer fish soup is the main dish.)

1397
00:53:19,242 --> 00:53:20,282
This is insane.

1398
00:53:21,342 --> 00:53:22,551
It's the best breakfast.

1399
00:53:22,552 --> 00:53:24,012
It's a great breakfast meal.

1400
00:53:25,352 --> 00:53:27,422
(It looks amazing.)

1401
00:53:28,452 --> 00:53:30,422
(Is she mixing the ssamjang with the chojang?)

1402
00:53:31,152 --> 00:53:32,762
(She takes a piece of the raw silver pomfret...)

1403
00:53:33,792 --> 00:53:35,461
(and dips it in the sauce she mixed together.)

1404
00:53:35,462 --> 00:53:37,492
(Eating)

1405
00:53:38,202 --> 00:53:39,701
When I eat raw fish,

1406
00:53:39,702 --> 00:53:42,301
I enjoy mixing up the sauces.

1407
00:53:42,302 --> 00:53:43,401
Right. People from the Gyeongsang Province...

1408
00:53:43,402 --> 00:53:44,471
eat like that.

1409
00:53:44,472 --> 00:53:45,901
- It's the same with sundae. - Right.

1410
00:53:45,902 --> 00:53:47,301
It's mostly chojang for Jeolla Province.

1411
00:53:47,302 --> 00:53:48,312
Right.

1412
00:53:48,342 --> 00:53:49,611
Even sundae is dipped in chojang.

1413
00:53:49,612 --> 00:53:50,812
- Right. - Right.

1414
00:53:51,112 --> 00:53:53,042
- Sundae dipped in chojang? - In Jeolla Province.

1415
00:53:53,342 --> 00:53:55,011
Cafeterias serve it like that.

1416
00:53:55,012 --> 00:53:56,082
- Really? - Also,

1417
00:53:56,312 --> 00:53:58,121
when talking with people from Busan,

1418
00:53:58,122 --> 00:54:01,022
they don't understand people from Seoul eating sundae with salt.

1419
00:54:01,182 --> 00:54:02,652
It's ssamjang no matter what for them.

1420
00:54:03,022 --> 00:54:04,991
Do you put in salt or sugar when having soybean noodles?

1421
00:54:04,992 --> 00:54:06,021
It's salt for me.

1422
00:54:06,022 --> 00:54:07,092
Both of them must be put in.

1423
00:54:07,422 --> 00:54:08,531
- Really? - You put in sugar, right?

1424
00:54:08,532 --> 00:54:10,832
I put in a whole bowl of sugar.

1425
00:54:11,362 --> 00:54:12,532
- A whole bowl? - Right.

1426
00:54:12,832 --> 00:54:15,572
- A whole bowl? - I put in a lot of sugar.

1427
00:54:15,702 --> 00:54:17,772
- You put in salt, right, Dowoon? - I don't like soybean noodles.

1428
00:54:20,472 --> 00:54:22,212
- That's understandable. - Right.

1429
00:54:23,312 --> 00:54:25,242
(She tries a spoonful of the puffer fish soup this time.)

1430
00:54:27,612 --> 00:54:28,981
(Slurping)

1431
00:54:28,982 --> 00:54:30,012
(Letting out a breath)

1432
00:54:32,352 --> 00:54:34,722
(This is it.)

1433
00:54:35,122 --> 00:54:36,152
She got touched by it.

1434
00:54:40,392 --> 00:54:43,092
(She grabs the vinegar all of a sudden.)

1435
00:54:43,562 --> 00:54:45,632
- The vinegar is placed like that. - Right.

1436
00:54:47,102 --> 00:54:49,002
Vinegar is added to the soup?

1437
00:54:50,402 --> 00:54:52,672
When a few drops of vinegar is added,

1438
00:54:52,802 --> 00:54:54,642
it brings up a savory taste.

1439
00:54:54,972 --> 00:54:57,612
It's charming how you can develop its taste.

1440
00:54:58,312 --> 00:54:59,841
- After eating around half of it? - Right.

1441
00:54:59,842 --> 00:55:01,041
After enjoying its refreshing taste.

1442
00:55:01,042 --> 00:55:02,051
Right.

1443
00:55:02,052 --> 00:55:04,451
Just like how the raw fish tastes savory...

1444
00:55:04,452 --> 00:55:06,452
because of the sushi rice,

1445
00:55:06,722 --> 00:55:09,492
the soup becomes tastier.

1446
00:55:12,392 --> 00:55:14,592
(Happy)

1447
00:55:15,762 --> 00:55:16,762
(She downs the soup from the bowl to finish off.)

1448
00:55:16,763 --> 00:55:18,832
The soup was so good.

1449
00:55:19,702 --> 00:55:21,262
(Groaning)

1450
00:55:22,202 --> 00:55:23,831
(Clean bowl!)

1451
00:55:23,832 --> 00:55:25,001
Gosh, she finished the whole bowl.

1452
00:55:25,002 --> 00:55:26,102
Clean bowl!

1453
00:55:26,972 --> 00:55:28,312
A nap would be perfect after that.

1454
00:55:29,542 --> 00:55:31,272
- A nap would be great. - Right.

1455
00:55:31,682 --> 00:55:33,682
The guys of Hwagae Market have really fallen for her.

1456
00:55:33,942 --> 00:55:35,282
- Right. - Right.

1457
00:55:36,852 --> 00:55:38,952
- That was great. - Thank you.

1458
00:55:39,622 --> 00:55:41,852
- I saw you on TV. - Really?

1459
00:55:42,022 --> 00:55:43,822
You're so pretty.

1460
00:55:43,892 --> 00:55:45,492
- Thank you. - She saw her on TV.

1461
00:55:45,722 --> 00:55:48,232
I went there really often as I'm a regular.

1462
00:55:48,462 --> 00:55:50,662
- She finally noticed her. - She noticed me for the first time.

1463
00:55:50,962 --> 00:55:52,401
- I'll come again. - Could I get a picture?

1464
00:55:52,402 --> 00:55:54,702
- Of course. - An autograph as well.

1465
00:55:54,902 --> 00:55:57,431
- I don't have an autograph. - You don't?

1466
00:55:57,432 --> 00:55:59,942
- You can make one today. - Sounds good.

1467
00:56:00,002 --> 00:56:01,671
I only signed things off when I used my card.

1468
00:56:01,672 --> 00:56:02,972
She's lifting her up.

1469
00:56:03,912 --> 00:56:05,882
I felt so embarrassed as I didn't have an autograph.

1470
00:56:07,142 --> 00:56:08,452
What must I do?

1471
00:56:09,597 --> 00:56:11,257
Sign it this way.

1472
00:56:11,282 --> 00:56:13,451
- I'm left-handed. - My daughter is also left-handed.

1473
00:56:13,452 --> 00:56:14,521
Really?

1474
00:56:14,522 --> 00:56:17,952
(Under the owner's lead, she tries again.)

1475
00:56:19,122 --> 00:56:20,491
(Looking around)

1476
00:56:20,492 --> 00:56:22,692
It's March 11 today.

1477
00:56:23,862 --> 00:56:24,862
(Thanks to the owner's perfect coaching...)

1478
00:56:24,863 --> 00:56:25,901
Thank you.

1479
00:56:25,902 --> 00:56:27,931
This is so embarrassing.

1480
00:56:27,932 --> 00:56:29,471
"Kim Si Hyeon was here!"

1481
00:56:29,472 --> 00:56:30,671
(Kim Si Hyeon was here.)

1482
00:56:30,672 --> 00:56:31,801
(I really enjoyed the food!)

1483
00:56:31,802 --> 00:56:33,842
Anyone could copy that.

1484
00:56:34,542 --> 00:56:36,472
Did you sign a guest book?

1485
00:56:36,772 --> 00:56:38,241
But I think that looks more charming.

1486
00:56:38,242 --> 00:56:39,442
- Really? - I like it.

1487
00:56:41,112 --> 00:56:42,651
(They take a picture together as well.)

1488
00:56:42,652 --> 00:56:43,882
Thank you.

1489
00:56:44,052 --> 00:56:45,152
Thank you.

1490
00:56:45,952 --> 00:56:46,952
I enjoy the food every time I come.

1491
00:56:46,982 --> 00:56:49,021
Thank you! It was great!

1492
00:56:49,022 --> 00:56:50,762
- Bye! Thank you! - Bye!

1493
00:56:52,762 --> 00:56:53,792
Where is she heading to now?

1494
00:56:54,592 --> 00:56:56,632
It doesn't seem like she will take the train back to Seoul.

1495
00:56:57,062 --> 00:56:58,962
(She starts running all of a sudden.)

1496
00:56:59,762 --> 00:57:00,831
It looks like she's running after spotting her mom.

1497
00:57:00,832 --> 00:57:02,271
- She looks so bright. - Right.

1498
00:57:02,272 --> 00:57:03,371
"Mom!"

1499
00:57:03,372 --> 00:57:06,702
(She runs like a child, feeling happy to see her mother.)

1500
00:57:08,142 --> 00:57:09,142
What is this place?

1501
00:57:09,167 --> 00:57:11,237
It must be a place for cooking. There are ingredients.

1502
00:57:11,312 --> 00:57:12,742
(She walks up naturally.)

1503
00:57:13,682 --> 00:57:16,081
Teacher! Hello!

1504
00:57:16,082 --> 00:57:18,781
Si Hyeon! What is all this?

1505
00:57:18,782 --> 00:57:19,821
I bought some things at the market.

1506
00:57:19,822 --> 00:57:20,822
- Who is it? - Who is it?

1507
00:57:20,823 --> 00:57:22,292
- You went to the market? - Right.

1508
00:57:22,337 --> 00:57:23,866
What is all this?

1509
00:57:23,867 --> 00:57:25,066
I bought some things at the market.

1510
00:57:25,067 --> 00:57:26,067
- Who is it? - Who is it?

1511
00:57:26,068 --> 00:57:27,136
- You went to the market? - Right.

1512
00:57:27,137 --> 00:57:28,236
It must be her cooking teacher.

1513
00:57:28,237 --> 00:57:29,306
Right.

1514
00:57:29,307 --> 00:57:30,777
- You came from the market? - Awesome.

1515
00:57:33,061 --> 00:57:34,230
There's a place like this?

1516
00:57:34,231 --> 00:57:36,271
(A place surrounded by all kinds of tableware and tables)

1517
00:57:36,531 --> 00:57:40,000
The place that I visited is the workroom of Lee Sang Hee,

1518
00:57:40,001 --> 00:57:41,941
a food researcher of Tongyeong food.

1519
00:57:44,571 --> 00:57:46,211
(He researched Tongyeong's food for around 40 years.)

1520
00:57:46,692 --> 00:57:48,392
(The master of Tongyeong's native food)

1521
00:57:49,762 --> 00:57:50,892
Food researcher?

1522
00:57:51,892 --> 00:57:53,031
It's still cold.

1523
00:57:53,032 --> 00:57:54,091
- Right. - You went to the market...

1524
00:57:54,092 --> 00:57:55,278
when it's still cold.

1525
00:57:56,308 --> 00:57:57,308
(Biting)

1526
00:57:57,708 --> 00:57:59,277
(Hugging)

1527
00:57:59,278 --> 00:58:01,178
The one next to him is his wife.

1528
00:58:01,478 --> 00:58:04,048
She's my favorite aunt.

1529
00:58:05,154 --> 00:58:07,084
I think it was when I was around 20.

1530
00:58:07,318 --> 00:58:11,057
I happened to visit Tongyeong.

1531
00:58:11,058 --> 00:58:14,558
I tried his food...

1532
00:58:15,128 --> 00:58:17,658
and I felt amazed by him and Tongyeong.

1533
00:58:17,858 --> 00:58:19,868
I got so mesmerized...

1534
00:58:20,068 --> 00:58:23,268
and as I wanted to learn how to make Tongyeong's food,

1535
00:58:23,438 --> 00:58:25,108
I went to meet him.

1536
00:58:25,338 --> 00:58:26,437
The food you tried was so good...

1537
00:58:26,438 --> 00:58:27,908
- and you decided to learn from him? - Right.

1538
00:58:28,068 --> 00:58:29,477
It's just like "Shota no Sushi."

1539
00:58:29,478 --> 00:58:31,077
- Right. - "Shota no Sushi."

1540
00:58:31,078 --> 00:58:32,477
- It's just like a comic book. - That was how good it was?

1541
00:58:32,478 --> 00:58:33,848
- That's amazing. - That's impressive.

1542
00:58:34,008 --> 00:58:36,478
That's the courage that comes out when you're crazy for something.

1543
00:58:36,778 --> 00:58:38,078
How good was it for her to be like that?

1544
00:58:38,688 --> 00:58:40,987
I said that I wanted to talk with him...

1545
00:58:40,988 --> 00:58:43,487
and asked for some of his time.

1546
00:58:43,488 --> 00:58:46,858
He gladly accepted me after hearing that...

1547
00:58:47,288 --> 00:58:49,258
I was a student learning to become a cook.

1548
00:58:50,128 --> 00:58:52,897
I said I wanted to follow him to the market,

1549
00:58:52,898 --> 00:58:54,327
so we went together the next day.

1550
00:58:54,328 --> 00:58:56,368
This is a picture taken by him.

1551
00:58:57,538 --> 00:58:59,308
I asked him to teach me how to make side dishes...

1552
00:58:59,508 --> 00:59:01,538
and I learned how to make Tongyeong's food from him.

1553
00:59:03,908 --> 00:59:05,748
- He's a great teacher. - Right.

1554
00:59:06,148 --> 00:59:07,508
And as time passed by,

1555
00:59:07,908 --> 00:59:09,548
we ended up becoming this close.

1556
00:59:09,848 --> 00:59:10,977
She must feel like a daughter to them.

1557
00:59:10,978 --> 00:59:12,388
Warm up your hands.

1558
00:59:13,148 --> 00:59:15,558
The things that are on the wall are all made by him.

1559
00:59:15,988 --> 00:59:17,118
- The sobans? - Yes.

1560
00:59:17,358 --> 00:59:19,088
- He studies food and culture. - Right.

1561
00:59:19,458 --> 00:59:21,528
- He studies food and culture. - Right.

1562
00:59:21,658 --> 00:59:22,658
Soban?

1563
00:59:23,428 --> 00:59:25,098
- What is a soban? - Soban?

1564
00:59:25,598 --> 00:59:26,768
Isn't it a bowl?

1565
00:59:27,428 --> 00:59:30,098
- A small bowl? - I think so.

1566
00:59:30,398 --> 00:59:31,808
You should ask.

1567
00:59:32,638 --> 00:59:33,867
Hurry up and ask.

1568
00:59:33,868 --> 00:59:35,338
What are sobans?

1569
00:59:35,378 --> 00:59:36,577
- Sobans... - What?

1570
00:59:36,578 --> 00:59:38,548
are the small tables that are on the wall.

1571
00:59:38,808 --> 00:59:40,208
Those are traditional sobans.

1572
00:59:40,678 --> 00:59:42,217
Have you not heard of dog-legged sobans?

1573
00:59:42,218 --> 00:59:43,347
- No. - The dog-legged ones.

1574
00:59:43,348 --> 00:59:44,977
Kian84 wanted me to ask.

1575
00:59:44,978 --> 00:59:46,217
(Kian84 wanted me to ask.)

1576
00:59:46,218 --> 00:59:47,348
They're those small tables.

1577
00:59:47,788 --> 00:59:49,487
You can't say that, you punk!

1578
00:59:49,488 --> 00:59:50,488
(Embarrassed)

1579
00:59:50,718 --> 00:59:52,227
Since you were coming,

1580
00:59:52,228 --> 00:59:53,728
I was planning to dig out some siler divaricata.

1581
00:59:53,928 --> 00:59:56,228
- That sounds great. - I was planning to do that.

1582
00:59:56,558 --> 00:59:57,957
They'll head to dig out vegetables?

1583
00:59:57,958 --> 00:59:59,168
They will dig them out?

1584
00:59:59,268 --> 01:00:00,298
- Shall we head out? - Yes.

1585
01:00:02,038 --> 01:00:03,068
You punk.

1586
01:00:03,298 --> 01:00:05,807
- There's an actual beast. - Right.

1587
01:00:05,808 --> 01:00:07,268
That cat was also abandoned.

1588
01:00:07,338 --> 01:00:08,338
(My goodness!)

1589
01:00:08,339 --> 01:00:10,508
The cat found a great place to stay. There must be great food there.

1590
01:00:12,878 --> 01:00:14,048
Let's get going.

1591
01:00:14,448 --> 01:00:15,848
I want to go out to dig out vegetables.

1592
01:00:16,848 --> 01:00:18,788
I should be able to distinguish the vegetables well.

1593
01:00:19,148 --> 01:00:21,218
Don't worry. Once you get there,

1594
01:00:21,858 --> 01:00:24,087
you will tell right away as you've studied them.

1595
01:00:24,088 --> 01:00:25,088
Right.

1596
01:00:25,758 --> 01:00:27,257
(After driving past Tongyeong's sea...)

1597
01:00:27,258 --> 01:00:28,288
Gosh, that's great.

1598
01:00:32,228 --> 01:00:33,398
It's this field.

1599
01:00:34,368 --> 01:00:36,068
- There are vegetables here. - I love it!

1600
01:00:36,938 --> 01:00:37,968
Awesome!

1601
01:00:38,298 --> 01:00:39,407
- A vegetable garden? - Yes.

1602
01:00:39,408 --> 01:00:40,637
(They arrive at the spot.)

1603
01:00:40,638 --> 01:00:42,178
Gosh, it's amazing!

1604
01:00:42,508 --> 01:00:44,608
- It's like a work of art. - That's what Tongyeong is about.

1605
01:00:44,738 --> 01:00:45,878
It's beautiful, isn't it?

1606
01:00:46,148 --> 01:00:47,788
Is that a seaweed farm?

1607
01:00:47,848 --> 01:00:49,718
That must be for either oysters or mussels.

1608
01:00:52,088 --> 01:00:53,887
- The view is amazing. - Right? It's amazing, right?

1609
01:00:53,888 --> 01:00:55,487
- There are many fishing boats. - You want to go fishing, right?

1610
01:00:55,488 --> 01:00:56,617
I know, of course.

1611
01:00:56,618 --> 01:00:58,058
I've been out fishing before as well.

1612
01:00:58,458 --> 01:00:59,828
It's close to Geoje.

1613
01:01:00,158 --> 01:01:01,287
(Laughing)

1614
01:01:01,288 --> 01:01:02,557
He looks so happy.

1615
01:01:02,558 --> 01:01:04,398
(It's great to see the sea of Gyeongsang Province.)

1616
01:01:04,528 --> 01:01:07,568
A friend of mine raises vegetables here.

1617
01:01:07,828 --> 01:01:09,738
- Awesome. Shall we go down? - Let's go down.

1618
01:01:10,338 --> 01:01:13,108
The vegetables must feel nervous.

1619
01:01:13,668 --> 01:01:15,838
- Death is right at their doorstep. - Right.

1620
01:01:17,978 --> 01:01:19,948
- Their hair will be pulled out. - It might happen any second.

1621
01:01:20,208 --> 01:01:21,918
(Watch out for your hair.)

1622
01:01:22,948 --> 01:01:24,778
- Look here, Si Hyeon. - Right.

1623
01:01:25,388 --> 01:01:26,917
Be careful as it's slippery.

1624
01:01:26,918 --> 01:01:29,717
- You see the clam shells, right? - Yes.

1625
01:01:29,718 --> 01:01:32,958
All of these are used as fertilizers without being thrown out.

1626
01:01:33,388 --> 01:01:34,827
- Really? - They are used as fertilizers.

1627
01:01:34,828 --> 01:01:36,328
- Clam shells? - Right.

1628
01:01:36,698 --> 01:01:38,097
So that is why they are found in places like that.

1629
01:01:38,098 --> 01:01:39,127
Right.

1630
01:01:39,128 --> 01:01:41,727
I always thought those places were beaches in the past.

1631
01:01:41,728 --> 01:01:42,768
What?

1632
01:01:43,598 --> 01:01:45,207
Then, trilobites from the past would be found as well.

1633
01:01:45,208 --> 01:01:47,807
Clam shells could be found like that in the countryside.

1634
01:01:47,808 --> 01:01:49,638
- They're all brought over. - Right.

1635
01:01:49,838 --> 01:01:50,908
I see.

1636
01:01:51,408 --> 01:01:52,708
- This is the leaf mustard. - This is the leaf mustard.

1637
01:01:53,608 --> 01:01:54,808
- Leaf mustard? - Leaf mustard.

1638
01:01:55,078 --> 01:01:56,778
- For leaf mustard kimchi. - Leaf mustard kimchi?

1639
01:01:57,118 --> 01:01:58,318
- It's made from that? - I see.

1640
01:01:58,918 --> 01:02:00,517
- Really? - This is the green leaf mustard.

1641
01:02:00,518 --> 01:02:02,917
The red leaf mustard is often used.

1642
01:02:02,918 --> 01:02:04,257
- The red ones. - Yeosu's leaf mustard kimchi...

1643
01:02:04,258 --> 01:02:05,857
- is made with the red leaf mustard. - Right.

1644
01:02:05,858 --> 01:02:08,357
All of those look precious after knowing about them.

1645
01:02:08,358 --> 01:02:10,327
Exactly. We would've just trampled over them.

1646
01:02:10,328 --> 01:02:11,898
Right. We would've just trampled over them.

1647
01:02:13,898 --> 01:02:15,397
Open your eyes wide and this place is full of treasure.

1648
01:02:15,398 --> 01:02:16,438
Right.

1649
01:02:16,568 --> 01:02:18,238
This place is great.

1650
01:02:18,468 --> 01:02:19,908
We have to know what's what for it to be treasure.

1651
01:02:22,108 --> 01:02:23,637
- You see the meogu, right? - Right.

1652
01:02:23,638 --> 01:02:25,447
- The meogu. - These are all meogu leaves.

1653
01:02:25,448 --> 01:02:26,608
- They noticed right away. - Right.

1654
01:02:27,748 --> 01:02:29,177
- I can't tell at all. - I can't tell at all.

1655
01:02:29,178 --> 01:02:30,377
- Are they butterbur leaves? - Meogu leaves.

1656
01:02:30,378 --> 01:02:33,548
Butterbur leaves are called meogu leaves in the dialect.

1657
01:02:33,648 --> 01:02:35,787
- So those are butterbur leaves. - Right.

1658
01:02:35,788 --> 01:02:37,017
- No one knew. - They're good.

1659
01:02:37,018 --> 01:02:38,257
- They just look like fallen leaves. - They don't taste bitter.

1660
01:02:38,258 --> 01:02:39,458
How can you call them fallen leaves?

1661
01:02:39,658 --> 01:02:41,088
Seafood is all you know about.

1662
01:02:41,588 --> 01:02:43,058
- Seafood is all you know about. - But...

1663
01:02:43,198 --> 01:02:46,228
they look like ginkgo leaves.

1664
01:02:46,498 --> 01:02:47,567
Right.

1665
01:02:47,568 --> 01:02:48,967
- Are they good for anything? - Good for anything?

1666
01:02:48,968 --> 01:02:50,337
- It's probably blood circulation. - Well...

1667
01:02:50,338 --> 01:02:51,537
It prevents arteriosclerosis...

1668
01:02:51,538 --> 01:02:53,038
- and it's good for antioxidation. - Antioxidation?

1669
01:02:54,968 --> 01:02:56,678
(The messenger of spring, butterbur)

1670
01:02:57,238 --> 01:02:58,377
(Butterbur: Antioxidation, blood sugar, blood pressure)

1671
01:02:58,378 --> 01:02:59,478
- It's right there! - Blood pressure regulation.

1672
01:02:59,748 --> 01:03:01,308
You're right. You just made a guess, didn't you?

1673
01:03:01,648 --> 01:03:02,877
Most of them...

1674
01:03:02,878 --> 01:03:04,677
- are good for the same things. - That's fascinating.

1675
01:03:04,678 --> 01:03:05,718
Right.

1676
01:03:07,218 --> 01:03:08,218
(What was that?)

1677
01:03:09,058 --> 01:03:12,357
(Eaten as a salad or wrap)

1678
01:03:12,358 --> 01:03:14,488
They taste great as wraps.

1679
01:03:14,858 --> 01:03:16,828
They don't taste bitter.

1680
01:03:17,228 --> 01:03:19,798
They can be eaten right away after being blanched.

1681
01:03:21,398 --> 01:03:22,467
- This is amazing. - They taste great...

1682
01:03:22,468 --> 01:03:23,538
in doenjang jjigae.

1683
01:03:23,768 --> 01:03:24,967
- How are those found? - Right.

1684
01:03:24,968 --> 01:03:26,007
This is amazing.

1685
01:03:26,008 --> 01:03:27,267
It looks like they are looking for four-leaved clovers.

1686
01:03:27,268 --> 01:03:28,538
It really feels like that.

1687
01:03:28,638 --> 01:03:30,337
- Time flies like that. - That's fine as well.

1688
01:03:30,338 --> 01:03:31,378
Right.

1689
01:03:32,208 --> 01:03:33,908
- Do you know what this is? - Which one?

1690
01:03:34,748 --> 01:03:36,917
A flower blossomed.

1691
01:03:36,918 --> 01:03:38,688
- It looks so pretty. - It's a flower.

1692
01:03:39,788 --> 01:03:42,458
- These come out before mugwort. - This is insane!

1693
01:03:42,618 --> 01:03:44,658
These come out before mugwort.

1694
01:03:45,218 --> 01:03:46,788
I've finally got my hands on this.

1695
01:03:49,728 --> 01:03:51,797
It's the henbit. It's the one I looked for...

1696
01:03:51,798 --> 01:03:53,227
- in Gyeongdong Market. - The henbit.

1697
01:03:53,228 --> 01:03:54,228
The henbit?

1698
01:03:54,229 --> 01:03:55,467
Right.

1699
01:03:55,468 --> 01:03:57,097
(The vegetable she looked for in Gyeongdong Market...)

1700
01:03:57,098 --> 01:03:58,968
(last winter!)

1701
01:04:00,438 --> 01:04:02,207
(Henbit: Antioxidation, hemostatic effect)

1702
01:04:02,208 --> 01:04:03,578
- Antioxidation again. - Antioxidation again.

1703
01:04:04,408 --> 01:04:05,847
I'm sure it will prevent arteriosclerosis as well.

1704
01:04:05,848 --> 01:04:08,348
(There's a big chance for that.)

1705
01:04:08,578 --> 01:04:11,947
(It's eaten as a salad or put in doenjang soup.)

1706
01:04:11,948 --> 01:04:12,948
Gosh, that must be delicious!

1707
01:04:12,949 --> 01:04:14,587
- Doenjang soup? - In doenjang soup.

1708
01:04:14,588 --> 01:04:15,858
(It's eaten as a salad or put in doenjang soup.)

1709
01:04:15,988 --> 01:04:17,158
It's so pretty.

1710
01:04:19,558 --> 01:04:21,288
This one feels different.

1711
01:04:22,328 --> 01:04:23,997
(She uses it as an accessory.)

1712
01:04:23,998 --> 01:04:25,728
I wonder it's called the clown vegetable in Korean.

1713
01:04:25,828 --> 01:04:27,227
As the flower looks so pretty,

1714
01:04:27,228 --> 01:04:28,967
- it was given that name. - As it resembles a clown?

1715
01:04:28,968 --> 01:04:30,167
- That's how I know it. - As it's very flashy?

1716
01:04:30,168 --> 01:04:31,368
(A name given because of its beautiful flower!)

1717
01:04:31,598 --> 01:04:33,638
I wish to have a garden just like this.

1718
01:04:34,438 --> 01:04:37,578
I've tried digging out vegetables at a place planted with crops,

1719
01:04:37,908 --> 01:04:39,707
but it's my first time experiencing

1720
01:04:39,708 --> 01:04:41,877
a wild field where things are grown randomly.

1721
01:04:41,878 --> 01:04:44,218
It was a fascinating experience.

1722
01:04:46,048 --> 01:04:48,347
(They move to the second spot.)

1723
01:04:48,348 --> 01:04:50,558
- Awesome! - Here.

1724
01:04:51,288 --> 01:04:52,757
- They don't grow in big fields. - What's that?

1725
01:04:52,758 --> 01:04:53,787
- What is that? - The siler divaricata?

1726
01:04:53,788 --> 01:04:54,858
They grow naturally like this.

1727
01:04:56,128 --> 01:04:58,528
- Last year, it started growing. - It's the siler divaricata.

1728
01:04:58,728 --> 01:05:00,367
You know so much!

1729
01:05:00,368 --> 01:05:01,398
The siler divaricata?

1730
01:05:03,538 --> 01:05:05,297
(Siler divaricata: A vegetable that prevents winds)

1731
01:05:05,298 --> 01:05:06,668
It prevents winds?

1732
01:05:06,808 --> 01:05:08,237
- That's why it's called fang feng. - It prevents winds.

1733
01:05:08,238 --> 01:05:10,138
- It prevents winds. - That's how it got that name.

1734
01:05:10,338 --> 01:05:11,408
Fang feng.

1735
01:05:12,448 --> 01:05:13,507
(Blanched or mixed as a salad)

1736
01:05:13,508 --> 01:05:14,948
- Gosh, that looks good! - Right.

1737
01:05:15,648 --> 01:05:17,617
- We must have tried it. - Right.

1738
01:05:17,618 --> 01:05:18,687
We would've tried it without knowing what it is.

1739
01:05:18,688 --> 01:05:20,088
We would've all tried that.

1740
01:05:20,688 --> 01:05:23,257
Goats know what tastes good. Check the scent.

1741
01:05:23,258 --> 01:05:24,387
(She takes a bite of the freshly picked siler divaricata.)

1742
01:05:24,388 --> 01:05:25,728
The scent can be felt right away.

1743
01:05:26,158 --> 01:05:28,157
- I love its slightly tough texture. - It's so good.

1744
01:05:28,158 --> 01:05:29,398
- Right. - Awesome!

1745
01:05:29,898 --> 01:05:31,957
It's hard to find this kind of siler divaricata.

1746
01:05:31,958 --> 01:05:33,528
(The taste of the siler in season makes her exclaim right away.)

1747
01:05:34,328 --> 01:05:35,767
Hello!

1748
01:05:35,768 --> 01:05:37,797
- Welcome. - Greet her.

1749
01:05:37,798 --> 01:05:39,307
It's the owner of the vegetable garden.

1750
01:05:39,308 --> 01:05:41,267
(The owner allowed them to dig out the vegetables.)

1751
01:05:41,268 --> 01:05:43,237
We'll dig out some of the spinach.

1752
01:05:43,238 --> 01:05:44,907
Dig out the big ones as much as you want.

1753
01:05:44,908 --> 01:05:46,048
The spinach.

1754
01:05:48,648 --> 01:05:49,977
These taste good.

1755
01:05:49,978 --> 01:05:51,678
(They start hunting for the spinach raised in the sea breeze.)

1756
01:05:53,548 --> 01:05:54,788
Check out the roots.

1757
01:05:57,288 --> 01:05:58,887
- I can tell those right away. - Right.

1758
01:05:58,888 --> 01:06:00,428
- Those are seen often. - The roots look familiar.

1759
01:06:02,128 --> 01:06:03,627
- They're so pretty. - Right.

1760
01:06:03,628 --> 01:06:06,327
(Her hands keep moving at the sight of the appetizing color of pink.)

1761
01:06:06,328 --> 01:06:07,967
I want to live like this in the countryside.

1762
01:06:07,968 --> 01:06:09,337
(Her hands keep moving at the sight of the appetizing color of pink.)

1763
01:06:09,338 --> 01:06:10,598
- Let's get just what we need. - Right.

1764
01:06:11,598 --> 01:06:13,108
It will never end when we start getting greedy.

1765
01:06:13,968 --> 01:06:15,638
Right. I'm starting to get greedy here.

1766
01:06:15,938 --> 01:06:18,178
Let's get just what we need for the day.

1767
01:06:18,378 --> 01:06:20,147
You start to get greedy and move all around, right?

1768
01:06:20,148 --> 01:06:21,547
(Just enough for today!)

1769
01:06:21,548 --> 01:06:23,918
You can pick out some of the crown daisy greens as well.

1770
01:06:24,048 --> 01:06:25,048
The crown daisy greens?

1771
01:06:25,049 --> 01:06:27,287
The crown daisy greens are good.

1772
01:06:27,288 --> 01:06:28,687
- Want some crown daisy greens? - Sounds good.

1773
01:06:28,688 --> 01:06:29,888
Let's head over to the crown daisy greens.

1774
01:06:30,488 --> 01:06:31,587
- What? - What?

1775
01:06:31,588 --> 01:06:32,628
- Let's have these. - Gosh!

1776
01:06:33,358 --> 01:06:34,927
Check out the scent.

1777
01:06:34,928 --> 01:06:36,257
(The green crown daisy greens have grown together nicely.)

1778
01:06:36,258 --> 01:06:38,358
They're the crown daisy greens raised under a pine tree.

1779
01:06:38,558 --> 01:06:39,598
Crown daisy greens.

1780
01:06:39,898 --> 01:06:40,997
The crown daisy greens look really nice.

1781
01:06:40,998 --> 01:06:42,768
- Could you move over a little bit? - Right.

1782
01:06:43,268 --> 01:06:44,567
I got greedy.

1783
01:06:44,568 --> 01:06:45,568
(I'm sorry.)

1784
01:06:45,569 --> 01:06:47,208
They taste great.

1785
01:06:49,038 --> 01:06:51,008
There's so much.

1786
01:06:51,438 --> 01:06:54,008
- But that's too much. - No, it's not.

1787
01:06:54,548 --> 01:06:55,918
You see, I like crown daisy.

1788
01:06:57,778 --> 01:07:00,117
- It seems he's greedier. - You're right.

1789
01:07:00,118 --> 01:07:01,948
(Joking)

1790
01:07:03,058 --> 01:07:04,218
Gosh, look over here.

1791
01:07:04,958 --> 01:07:06,058
I love it.

1792
01:07:07,358 --> 01:07:08,688
I think that's enough.

1793
01:07:08,958 --> 01:07:09,997
He's fast.

1794
01:07:09,998 --> 01:07:11,258
(He's suddenly gotten quicker.)

1795
01:07:11,898 --> 01:07:13,667
(The master who taught her...)

1796
01:07:13,668 --> 01:07:15,768
(not to be greedy and get only what she needed...)

1797
01:07:16,138 --> 01:07:17,237
Wait a little longer.

1798
01:07:17,238 --> 01:07:19,467
